\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  Joins Concerted  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 192 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 14:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO -(Dow Jones)- The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed that it joined the concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of the euro earlier Friday, but declined to comment further on the amounts of the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  it bought, or on other details of its actions. \par \par \uc2  A Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  spokesperson said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was roughly simultaneous with those of the other major central banks involved in the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; namely the U.S. Federal Reserve, the Bank of Japan, the Bank of England, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and several other European central banks. \par \par \uc2  Following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which took place shortly after 7:00 a.m. EDT (1100 GMT), the euro spiked sharply higher against the Canadian dollar, rising from the C$1.2950 area as far as C$1.3453 before retreating. \par \par \uc2  At 9:25 a.m. EDT (1325 GMT), the euro was trading at C$1.3210 against the Canadian dollar, and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said cross-selling pressures against the Canadian dollar from European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had eased considerably. \par \par \uc2  -Paul Evans, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2022; paulr.evans@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  4346 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020010805dw9m03l67\par }\page {\pa