\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Duisenberg sees weak euro an inflation risk. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jeffrey Jones \par \uc2 526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2000\par 20:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  CALGARY, Sept 8 (Reuters) - European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg on Friday expressed concern for the first time in months about the euro's sharp decline, saying it could spur inflation and undermine investor confidence in the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "A very prolonged position of the euro clearly below what represents its fundamental equilibrium - a prolonged position below that would have an impact on future domestic European inflation. That would be a matter of concern," Duisenberg said during a question session with business leaders. \par \par \uc2  It was imperative to maintain confidence in the still sliding euro, he added. The European common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has plunged more than 25 percent since its launch in January 1999. \par \par \uc2  Duisenberg spoke as the euro hovered near life lows against the dollar and as European finance ministers pleaded that a strong euro is in the interest of the entire region. \par \par \uc2  The euro plunged some four cents this week, hitting fresh life lows several times even as euro zone finance ministers, meeting in France, said growth remained very robust in the area. \par \par \uc2  The ministers also said the ECB remained committed to maintaining price stability, fearing a soft euro could push up domestic prices for goods like oil, which is denominated in dollars and is already soaring. \par \par \uc2  Speaking thousands of miles across the Atlantic, Europe's top central banker said the ECB's monetary policy goal is to keep European inflation in check rather than to target a specific exchange rate for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In his Calgary remarks, however, Duisenberg failed to address market speculation about how the ECB may act to stem the euro's persistent slide, such as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop it up. \par \par \uc2  Members of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's business and political elite attending the speech said they were still in the dark about the ECB's next step. \par \par \uc2  "I don't take what Duisenberg said as a signal that they may or may not take action," said Tony Comper, chief executive officer of Bank of Montreal, Canada's fifth largest bank. "Like everything else, things have to unfold and markets will react as they think appropriate." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have been inundated with talk about the euro this week, especially after Germany's Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder said earlier this week that he was unconcerned by its fall. Though he later took back the statement, the damage had already been done, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Our objective is to maintain stable prices in the euro area and not a specific level of the euro's exchange rate," said Duisenberg, speaking on the international role of the euro to the Spruce Meadows Round-table, an annual gathering of global corporate leaders in Canada. \par \par \uc2  In his speech, Duisenberg said international investors would have to consider the euro to be a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold assets in the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  "Only if investors outside the euro area are confident that their purchasing power will be preserved over time, will they engage in euro-denominated financial activities," he said. \par \par \uc2  His comments came as the euro hovered around $0.8654 - a little above its record lows against the dollar. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9808kei\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\pi