\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar drops sharply as U.S. dollar surges. \b0\par\par\uc2 789 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Mai 2000\par 23:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab  CANADA OPEN  1.4867    PREVIOUS CLOSE  1.4852\par \tab \tab  DAILY  HIGH  1.4940    OVERNIGHT HIGH  1.4894\par \tab \tab \tab    LOW  1.4850\tab \tab    LOW  1.4848\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2      TORONTO, May 3 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar again closed \par \par \uc2  sharply lower on Wednesday, hitting its lowest levels since \par \par \uc2  October as it joined other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in swooning before the \par \par \uc2  dominant U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Fears that the Bank of Canada won't be able to keep pace \par \par \uc2  with a widely expected 50-basis-point interest rate tightening \par \par \uc2  from the U.S. Federal Reserve later this month also played a \par \par \uc2  critical role in pushing the beleaguered Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  lower, market watchers said. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar closed at C$1.4932 (66.97 U.S. cents) \par \par \uc2  on Wednesday from C$1.4852 (67.33 U.S. cents) at the previous \par \par \uc2  session's close. It reached C$1.4940, breaching the C$1.4900 \par \par \uc2  level for the first time since October 20, before strengthening \par \par \uc2  modestly in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday's slump came after several sessions of sustained \par \par \uc2  decline for the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which closed at C$1.4743 on \par \par \uc2  April 19. \par \par \uc2  With market players increasingly convinced that the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Federal Reserve will raise rates by 50 basis points at its May \par \par \uc2  16 policy-setting meeting, and many doubting that the Canadian \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will fully match the move, fears about \par \par \uc2  interest-rate divergence played a major role in nudging the \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, market watchers said. \par \par \uc2      "I think that's the overwhelming story," said Doug Porter, \par \par \uc2  senior economist at Nesbitt Burns Inc. \par \par \uc2      But the global strength of the U.S. dollar, which helped \par \par \uc2  pushed the euro to record lows on Wednesday, is another central \par \par \uc2  reason for the Canadian unit's floundering performance, he \par \par \uc2  added. \par \par \uc2  "The other factor rumbling in the background is a more \par \par \uc2  widespread strengthening in the U.S. dollar," he said. "Canada \par \par \uc2  is not the only \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under pressure here, with the euro \par \par \uc2  touching lifetime lows and the Australian dollar extremely \par \par \uc2  weak." \par \par \uc2  Some buying of the Australian dollar against the Canadian \par \par \uc2  unit was actually credited with helping trigger the added \par \par \uc2  pressure on the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday, said one \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a major Canadian bank. \par \par \uc2      "After that, a U.S. investment type was seen buying (the \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar) through C$1.4900," the trader added. \par \par \uc2      "When we broke through C$1.4900, a psychological (level), \par \par \uc2  people probably tried to abandon any long Canada positions, to \par \par \uc2  just go short Canada and see what happens," he said. \par \par \uc2      While some market watchers suggested the Canadian dollar is \par \par \uc2  unlikely to weaken much beyond C$1.4900 in the near term, it \par \par \uc2  could easily tumble lower in coming sessions, the trader said. \par \par \uc2      "I think with it hovering around here, people are probably \par \par \uc2  looking for the (U.S.) dollar to test higher against the \par \par \uc2  Canadian dollar," he said. "I think people now are looking at \par \par \uc2  the C$1.5000 psychological level, as well. There's no reason we \par \par \uc2  can't have a peek at that." \par \par \uc2       The trader also agreed that the pervasive strength of the \par \par \uc2  U.S. dollar was buffeting the Canadian dollar on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2      "I think it was just a general strength in the U.S. dollar \par \par \uc2  across the board, and that contributed as well to Canada's \par \par \uc2  weakness," he said. \par \par \uc2      While some market participants are talking about the \par \par \uc2  possibility of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  markets to bolster the sagging Canadian unit, the trader \par \par \uc2  dismissed such speculation as quite implausible. \par \par \uc2  "I really don't think they are really in a position to \par \par \uc2  consider that at this point in time. The move seems to be \par \par \uc2  relatively orderly," the trader said. "I don't think there's a \par \par \uc2  sense of the panic in the market just yet, it's more a sense of \par \par \uc2  annoyance, more than anything." \par \par \uc2      Trading in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was heavy, with "good volume going \par \par \uc2  through," the trader said. \par \par \uc2      With fears of increased interest rate divergence between \par \par \uc2  Canada and the United States hovering over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \par \par \uc2  release of both Canadian and U.S. jobs data on Friday will be \par \par \uc2  crucial for the Canadian unit, market watchers said. \par \par \uc2  Forecasters are looking for a 358,000 increase in U.S. \par \par \uc2  nonfarm payrolls in April, and a 36,250 increase in Canadian \par \par \uc2  employment in the same month, according to consensus forecasts \par \par \uc2  prepared by Reuters. \par \par \uc2  In cross-trading against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Canadian \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollar was at 72.94 yen\tab   , compared with 72.95 at the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par previous session's close, and at C$1.3375 against the euro\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  versus C$1.3506. The Canadian dollar was at A$1.1291\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  against the Australian dollar versus A$1.1492.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Don Curren, Reuters Toronto Newsroom, 416 941-8100,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 2000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw5301vfw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
