\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar stays at 2-month high at midday. \b0\par\par\uc2 671 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 1999\par 18:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab     CANADA OPEN 1.4817     PREVIOUS  CLOSE 1.4783\par \tab     DAILY  HIGH 1.4828     OVERNIGHT HIGH  1.4828\par \tab \tab      LOW  1.4730\tab \tab    LOW   1.4770\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ---------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     TORONTO, Sept 10 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar\tab \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par jumped to a two-month high on Friday on signs of tame U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par inflation and rising energy and commodities prices.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  maintained most of its morning gains, drifting\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par around C$1.4740/50 at midday, a key technical level cracked in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par early trade.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was also posting gains in cross trading as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Tokyo slowed the yen's upward march and the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par euro continued to hit new lows.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Following Thursday's sharp gains, market players rushed to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par buy more Canadian dollars because they were relieved by\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par indications of benign U.S. inflation seen in wholesale price\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par data released on Friday and the employment report a week ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The data more than offset the negative impact of weak\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadian employment numbers released early on Friday, which had\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dented the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  briefly.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Now all eyes are on U.S. retail inflation data due out next\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wednesday, which should provide a good clue to whether U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policymakers will be inclined to raise interest\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rates at their next meeting on October 5.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The overall U.S. August producer price index came in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par stronger than forecast, but excluding volatile food and energy\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par prices, the index actually fell and calmed inflation jitters.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's an interesting ironic mix that you see strong U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economic news creates bearish asset markets and a weaker\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , whereas weaker U.S. economic news creates\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par bullish asset markets and a stronger Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Jack Spitz, regional manager, treasury financial markets, at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par National Bank.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "But that's not necessarily a long-term view because\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par fundamentals will always come back," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Rising prices in energy and commodities are a cause for\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par concern among fixed-income investors, but Canadian dollar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par buyers are paying more attention to the benefit of higher\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par commodities prices, which boost the value of Canada's exports\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par and ensure job creation down the road.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The closely watched Commodity Research Bureau (CRB) index\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par stayed just under 202, near a 10-month high.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     A drop in full-time jobs sent Canada's unemployment rate up\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to 7.8 percent in August from 7.7 percent in July, making the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par second consecutive monthly rise after unemployment hit a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par nine-year-low of 7.6 percent in June.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     There were 6,500 fewer jobs in Canada in August against the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par average forecast for an increase of 26,000.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Economists expect the employment report, which reflects\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economic conditions about six months ago, to have ups and down\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in the coming months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The first trigger for boosting Canada this week was news\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par from Japan. The Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had gained sharply on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thursday on brighter prospects for the Japanese economy seen in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par stronger-than-expected growth data for the April-June quarter,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par which promise to generate more demand for commodities exported\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par by Canada.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's dollar has blown away the summer blues. In much of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par July and August investors favoured U.S. dollar assets for higher\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par interest rates. The U.S. Federal Reserve raised short-term\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rates twice between late June and late August, while the Bank\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of Canada has maintained its easy money policy after lowering\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rates twice this year, with the last cut in early May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross-trading, Canada's dollar rebounded to 73.81 yen\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  from 72.99 yen at the previous close here, and was\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par firmer against the euro\tab    at C$1.5338 after C$1.5559.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Canadian dollar fell against the Australian dollar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    to A$1.0405 from A$1.0351.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The U.S. dollar popped up to around 109 yen after the Bank\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par of Japan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market to stem the yen's steep rise,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par which could undermine exporters' profits and overall economic\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par growth.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv9a00rlw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
