\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Morning: C$ Down As Rate Outlook Clouds Prospects \b0\par\par\uc2 453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1999\par 15:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1999, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar is lower amid relatively thin dealings early Friday, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to be pressured by expectations that with U.S. interest rates poised to move higher, the Bank of Canada will hold domestic rates steady, widening the interest rate premium for U.S. dollar-denominated assets. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.4650 at 9:42 a.m. EDT (1342 GMT), from C$1.4656 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT), and C$1.4619 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that with financial markets having taken U.S. Fed chairman Alan Greenspan's remarks on Thursday as confirmation of an imminent modest hike in U.S. interest rates, Friday's soft Canadian inflation data has also confirmed that there is no rationale for higher Canadian rates at present. \par \par \uc2  "The Canada has essentially been under pressure since Mr. Greenspan's comments yesterday," said John Nicholson, European manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the Bank of Nova Scotia in London. "The rather benign Canadian inflation numbers bring into the spotlight whether the Bank of Canada will match the Fed rate hike at the end of this month, and with inflation as low as it is, the answer is probably not." \par \par \uc2  Statistics Canada said early Friday that the consumer price index for all items rose 0.3% in May from April, while core CPI (excluding food and energy) rose 1.4% from the same month in 1998. \par \par \uc2  Traders in Canada said there was domestic interest to buy limited amounts of Canadian dollars as confirmation of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's buying of euros against yen made the rounds, but the move had little muscle behind it and the U.S. dollar soon returned to trade around C$1.4660. \par \par \uc2  "There were a few Canadians buying \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which allowed the Canadian dollar to blip to about C$1.4630," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a large Canadian bank in Montreal. "But they didn't succeed in pushing it past there, and it came right back." \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to continue grinding higher the rest of Friday, with a decisive break of the C$1.4660 area seen favoring a move past the C$1.4700 figure. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These are the exchange rates at 9:42 a.m. EDT (1342 GMT), 8:00 a.m.\par EDT (1200 GMT), and late Thursday.\par    USD/CAD    1.4650    1.4656    1.4619\par    EUR/CAD    1.5184    1.5225    1.5109\par    CAD/JPY    82.13     81.82     81.57\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Paul Evans; 416-306-2022; paulr.evans@dowjones.ca\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 18-06-99\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1348GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010826dv6i04kyj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
