\par\par \b \uc2  Canada's top central banker says  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  is undervalued. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Hopkins \par \uc2 648 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 1998\par 22:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  OTTAWA, Nov 16 (Reuters) - Bank of Canada Governor Gordon Thiessen gave a short-term boost to the Canadian dollar on Monday, departing from the cautious words of a policy report to scold investors for undervaluing the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I'm certainly not happy with the relative weakness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I think it's undervalued, and I think it should be stronger. But over short periods of time, markets do move around," Thiessen told a press conference after the release of the bank's semi-annual monetary policy report. \par \par \uc2  Canada's dollar strengthened to about 64.7 U.S. cents on the comments before sliding back to about 64.5 in late trade. \par \par \uc2  The report itself, released more than an hour before Thiessen spoke to the media, received a lukewarm response from financial markets. \par \par \uc2  In the carefully worded report, the Bank cut its projections for economic growth in Canada to "just below" three percent in 1998 and between 2.25 percent and 3.25 percent in 1999, citing the effects of the global financial storm. \par \par \uc2  Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in May had forecast annualized growth of about 3.5 percent through the middle of 1999. \par \par \uc2  Thiessen said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will pay "particular attention" to short-term confidence in \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's markets, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy or sell Canadian dollars or adjusting interest rates as needed to preserve confidence. \par \par \uc2  "We have to use the tools that we've got if we find ourselves confronted again with an undermining of confidence in markets," Thiessen said, referring the decline of Canada's dollar to record lows in August. \par \par \uc2  The bank spent about US$5 billion of its foreign reserves in August to try and shore up the weak dollar. This had little lasting effect and the bank was then forced to raise interest rates a full percentage point to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. \par \par \uc2  Thiessen said investors had exaggerated the effects of low commodity prices on Canada's economy, and oversold the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The central banker stressed that market flows are particularly thin because of the current global financial uncertainty, so fluctuations appear exaggerated. \par \par \uc2  "It doesn't take very much to move (\b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) in one direction or another. So I don't think you want to attach a huge amount of significance to short-term movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Thiessen told reporters. \par \par \uc2  In its report, the bank noted that the depreciation of the dollar against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  improves Canadian competitiveness in foreign markets and will likely boost export volumes and domestic demand, while decreasing the growth of imports. \par \par \uc2  The bank also expects growth of business investment and consumer demand to ease through to the end of next year. \par \par \uc2  Thiessen declined to offer specific projections on when Canada might achieve full production capacity. The bank noted that the margin of slack in Canada's economy has remained essentially unchanged since the end of 1997. \par \par \uc2  The bank had said in its May report that Canada should reach full capacity, closing its so-called output gap, during 1999. \par \par \uc2  Thiessen bristled at the suggestion that by immediately matching the last two interest rate cuts by the U.S. Federal Reserve, the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had made it easy for market players to bet against Canada's dollar. \par \par \uc2  "That presumes that we will always be predictable in the future, and I don't think you should presume that," he said. \par \par \uc2  The focus of the financial markets remains on U.S. monetary policy, with many expecting the U.S. Federal Reserve to cut interest rates on Tuesday by 0.25 of a percentage point to support the economy. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada is widely expected to follow quickly any move to lower rates unless the Canadian dollar is faltering. \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Ottawa Newsroom, 613-235-6745, fax 613-235-5890)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926dubg04i3t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
