\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  changed  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  policy-dealers. \b0\par\par\uc2 352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 1998\par 13:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 30 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  has abandoned its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Canadian dollar when it exceeds a certain range against the U.S. dollar, dealers in London said. \par \par \uc2  In a meeting last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to abandon its policy of buying or selling Canadian dollars on moves of more than half of one percent from the close of trading in Canada to the following day's open, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Canada has changed their policy," one dealer at a major Canadian bank in London. "It was half of one percent, but they have said they are no longer using that policy." \par \par \uc2  Massive spending by Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the Canadian dollar drained nearly $5 billion from the country's foreign reserves in August, leaving it looking to borrow to replenish its stores. \par \par \uc2  Canada's Finance Department said a record $5.82 billion was spent last month on government \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , mostly market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  spending. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy Canadian dollars on September 17. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar was under pressure on Wednesday after the Bank of Canada matched a 25 basis points cut in U.S. interest rates on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a U.S. name was attempting to test the Bank of Canada's resolve as the dollar weakened in the wake of the cut. \par \par \uc2  "The market and this U.S. name in particular wants to see where the Bank of Canada will come in and start defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if at all," said John Glover, managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Toronto-Dominion Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  At 1115 GMT the Canadian dollar was trading at C$1.5225/30 ($0.6565/68), just off a one-week low of C$1.5245 ($0.6559), but down more than one U.S. cent from C$1.4997/02 ($0.6665/68) in late Europe on Tuesday. ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, paul.casciato@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du9u010ej\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
