\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dlr weak as market tests BOC after rate cut. \b0\par\par\uc2 523 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 1998\par 14:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 30 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar hit its lowest levels in a week in European trading on Wednesday amid talk the market was attempting to test the Bank of Canada's resolve to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said a U.S. seller of Canadian dollars was attempting to find out whether and where the Bank of Canada would defend the Canadian dollar after the Bank was said to have abandoned its recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. \par \par \uc2  "The market and this U.S. name in particular wants to see where the Bank of Canada will come in and start defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if at all," said John Glover, managing director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Toronto-Dominion Bank in London. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said earlier that the Bank of Canada had told Canadian banks it has abandoned its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the dollar on moves of more than one half of a percent from the Canadian close to its open the following day. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Canada has changed their policy," one dealer at a major Canadian bank in London. "It was half of one percent, but they have said they are no longer using that policy." \par \par \uc2  Canada's dollar was also under pressure after the Bank of Canada matched a U.S. interest rate cut by trimming its bank rate to 5.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  The bank rate is the ceiling of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 50 basis points target range for the overnight financing rate, while its U.S. equivalent, the Fed funds rate was cut to 5.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  At 1115 GMT the Canadian dollar was trading at C$1.5225/30 ($0.6565/68), just off a one-week low of C$1.5245 ($0.6559), but down more than one U.S. cent from C$1.4997/02 ($0.6665/68) in late Europe on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Canada's Finance Department said earlier this month that a record $5.82 billion was spent in August on government \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , mostly market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  spending. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy Canadian dollars on September 17. \par \par \uc2  In other dollar bloc news, the Australian and New Zealand dollars were lifted momentarily by the U.S. interest rate cut. But a drop in Japanese stocks to a 12-1/2 year low reminded investors that Australia's biggest export market remained mired in recession. \par \par \uc2  Japanese stocks fell as capital repatriation dwindled at the nation's fiscal half-year and a Moody's Investors Service downgrade of Nomura Securities Co Ltd debt rating. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they expected more gloomy news out of Japan on Thursday when the Bank of Japan releases its quarterly business sentiment survey. \par \par \uc2  At 1205 GMT the Australian dollar was trading at $0.5942/47 compared with a high of $0.5991 overnight and $0.5966/71 in late Europe on Tuesday, while the New Zealand dollar was trading at $0.5017/24 compared with $0.4950/57 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Casciato, London newsroom +44 171 542 8968 fax +44 171 542 5293, paul.casciato@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du9u00yxx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
