\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Morning: C$ Tumbles As Mkt Reacts To BOC Rate Cut \b0\par\par\uc2 331 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 1998\par 15:16\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar is substantially lower Wednesday morning as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets respond to the Bank of Canada's decision to cut its benchmark bank rate 25 basis points to 5.75% Tuesday afternoon. \par \par \uc2  "The market is obviously very, very disappointed by what the bank did yesterday," said Bill Mooney, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Societe Generale. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.5280 at 9:08 a.m. EDT (1308 GMT), from C$1.5232 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT), and from C$1.5113 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The deterioration in the Canadian dollar started in earnest at around 4 p.m. Tuesday, with the U.S. dollar rising to the C$1.5160 level very late in North American trading. It weakened off early in the overnight session, but started rising again in London trading, reaching C$1.5246 at 8:26 a.m. EDT (1226 GMT). \par \par \uc2  News that Canadian gross domestic product dipped by 0.3% in July - the consensus forecast was for no change - pushed the Canadian dollar lower. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reached C$1.5300 shortly after Statistics Canada released the news at 8:30 a.m. EDT (1230 GMT) before edging back down slightly. \par \par \uc2  Market sources said the Canadian unit's abrupt descent triggered several stop loss orders, which exacerbated the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moving well below its normal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  trigger of C$1.5175. \par \par \uc2  Some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market sources said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has abandoned its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  every time the U.S. dollar moves 70 ticks or more from the previous day's close. \par \par \uc2  Traders expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selling U.S. dollars if the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  made a sustained breach of C$1.5300 Wednesday morning. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 30-09-98 \par \par \uc2  1316GMT \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du9u08foi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
