\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar hurt by Chretien interest rate gaffe. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Simao \par \uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1998\par 22:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - A stumbling foray into monetary policy by Canadian Prime Minister Jean Chretien set the stage for yet another dismal day for the troubled Canadian dollar on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar plunged three quarters of a cent to 65.33 U.S. cents in a second consecutive day of heavy losses as investors continued to react to Chretien's call for lower U.S. and Canadian interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The sharp decline prompted the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by buying Canadian dollars at levels from 65.74 to 65.33 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  Chretien, a former finance minister, made his remarks on Wednesday amid signs that U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan was not contemplating an imminent interest rate cut. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian prime minister told reporters in Ottawa yesterday that he hoped the U.S. would cut its interest rates and that this would "give room" to the Bank of Canada to do the same. \par \par \uc2  "Mr. Chretien's comments did raise the prospect that if the Fed were to ever lower rates, maybe the Bank of Canada not only would match but potentially could leapfrog. That is bothering the market," said Michael Gregory, senior economist at Lehman Brothers Inc. in New York. \par \par \uc2  Control over interest rates in Canada, as in many other countries, falls under the jurisdiction of an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than the government. It is the Bank of Canada's Governor Gordon Thiessen, not Chretien, who determines the direction of Canadian interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Aware that the prime minister had treaded in dangerous territory, Finance Minister Paul Martin told a gathering of the ruling Liberal Party in suburban Toronto on Thursday that he would not discuss the Canadian dollar or interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Others were less reluctant. \par \par \uc2  "I do think it is inappropriate. I don't think the prime minister of Canada should be calling on the Federal Reserve to ease interest rates. It gives all the wrong signals," said Sherry Cooper, chief economist at brokerage Nesbitt Burns in Toronto. \par \par \uc2  Chretien's misstep, combined with Greenspan's failure to signal lower interest rates in the United States, abruptly halted a three-week rally in which the Canadian dollar had gained almost four percent in value against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Chretien spoke to reporters shortly after a poker-faced Greenspan had failed to mention interest rates in testimony before U.S. lawmakers, kicking support out from under the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  The troubled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has benefited handsomely in recent weeks from growing market speculation that the U.S. Federal Reserve might soon cut interest rates to cope with the threat of global financial turmoil. Lower U.S. rates would tend boost the Canadian dollar because higher-yielding Canadian instruments would become more attractive to investors. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Greenspan's refusal to signal an impending rate cut had heightened anxiety over the fate of the global financial system and deflated some of the recent optimism that supported commodity-dependent \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "We are seeing a lingering impact of Greenspan's comments. There was a feeling out there that lower rates by the Fed were needed to turn the global economy around," Gregory said. \par \par \uc2  Questions about the direction and management of Canada's monetary policy were also swirling in markets. \par \par \uc2  Harsh criticism was leveled at Thiessen on August 27 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key interest rate by a full percentage point to 6.0 percent in a bid to shore up the flagging Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar came under pressure earlier this year, first from the Asian crisis and then from concerns weakening commodity prices would impair Canadian economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The August rate hike flew in the face of Thiessen's earlier pledge to hold monetary policy steady and prompted many economists to lower growth forecasts for the Canadian economy. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Thiessen would have a lot of explaining to do next week when he speaks in public for the first time since last month's rate hike. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.53 Canadian). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9h01dbc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
