\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  dollar stumbles as  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 413 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1998\par 22:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar closed sharply weaker at C$1.5305 (US$0.6538) in steady trading on Thursday after Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought Canadian dollars at levels from C$1.5210 (US$0.6574) to C$1.5305 (US$0.6533) as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  snapped through key resistance. \par \par \uc2  The Canada unit continued to suffer fallout from comments by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan and Canadian Prime Minister Jean Chretien. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan failed to signal a desire for higher U.S. interest rates during testimony to U.S. lawmakers on Wednesday, while Chretien called for lower U.S. and Canadian interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "Mr. Chretien's comments did raise the prospect that if the Fed were to ever lower rates, maybe the Bank of Canada not only would match but potentially could leapfrog. That is bothering the market," said Michael Gregory, senior economist with New York-based brokerage firm Lehman Brothers Inc. \par \par \uc2  Markets had speculated lower U.S. interest rates would be required to counter fears that global financial turmoil would dramatically slow U.S. economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The prospect of a U.S. rate cut, which makes some foreign higher-yielding investments in countries like Canada more attractive, had bolstered confidence in the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  The release of Canadian trade data also failed to underpin the weaker Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Statistics Canada reported Canada's merchandise trade surplus was C$1.55 billion in July in line with a consensus forecast of C$1.5 billion. \par \par \uc2  The government agency also sharply revised down the June surplus to C$0.9 billion from C$1.54 billion to take into account a large shipment of precious metals and alloys that should not have been included among Canadian exports. \par \par \uc2  "It's kind of a wash. It's mostly a result of the auto sector, which we expected," said Mario Angastiniotis, economist with Standard & Poor's MMS International in Toronto. \par \par \uc2  Angastiniotis, however, added that the June revision could lead to a revised current account deficit for the second quarter. \par \par \uc2  In cross trading, the Canadian dollar fell to 1.1080 German marks from Wednesday's close of 1.1168 German marks and dipped to 86.29 yen from 88.53 yen at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Simao, Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8104) or email: \par \par \uc2  toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9h01daz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
