\par\par \b \uc2  Toronto stocks down 2.5 pct at midday on Greenspan. \b0\par\par\uc2 549 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1998\par 18:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab     CHANGE\tab \tab \tab   CHANGE\par TSE300     5758.29 -147.29 HI 5797.70\tab    LO 5748.44\par DJIA\tab 7881.24 -208.54 GOLD (LONDON)   US$290.50  2.25\par FTSE\tab 5132.90 -158.80 GOLD (COMEX)   US$293.60 +3.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NIKKEI    13859.14 -338.56 LME CASH NICKEL US$3980    -38 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANDLR\tab 1.5240\tab  LME CASH ALUM   US$1321.5  -4.0\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CAN 30-YR   136.99   +0.42 BRENT CRUDE    US$13.52  +0.26\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------MARKET COMMENT-------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Toronto stocks fell 2.49 percent on Thursday after Federal Reserve Chairman\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Alan Greenspan killed hopes on Wednesday for a near-term co-oridinated rate cut\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par by industrialised nations. * The Canadian dollar continued its fall after Prime\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Minister Jean Chretien on Wednesday called for a cut in U.S. and Canadian\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates for the sake of the global economy. * Volume was moderate at\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 39.7 million shares traded worth C$750 million.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Toronto stocks stabilised at midday after shedding 2.7 percent within minutes of the opening bell on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The drop came after Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan torpedoed market hopes on Wednesday for a imminent rate cut by industrialised nations and Prime Minister Jean Chretien said he wished for a rate cut.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Toronto Stock Exchange's 300 Composite Index was down 147.29 points or 2.49 percent at midday at 5758.76. Volume was moderate at 39.7 million shares worth C$750.3 million. Declines far outnumbered gains by 544 to 184 with 169 issues unchanged.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar was sharply weaker at noon at C$1.5250 ($0.6555). The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  earlier in the day as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traded between C$1.5210 ($0.6574) and C$1.5240 ($0.6561)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Chretien has a knack for saying the wrong thing and the guys in the U.S. love to blow the (Canadian) dollar up when he talks," said Joe Canavan, president of Synergy Mutual Funds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In New York, the Dow Jones Industrial Average was down 208.54 points or 2.6 percent to 7881.24 at midday, also on Greenspan's dashing of rate-cut hopes.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thirteen of Toronto's 14 subgroups were down, with the heavily weighted banking sector 3.89 percent lower. Only the gold and precious metals subindex was higher, up 3.27 percent at midday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I'd be surprised if we saw any kind of a rally later in the session; I think the action's over for the day," said Conor Bill, director of retail trading at ScotiaMcleod.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par London, Paris, Frankfurt and Milan equities markets were dragged down after Tokyo dropped two percent to a 12-year low and Hong Kong posted steep losses after Greenspan's testimony.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Germany's Bundesbank said it will keep its discount, Lombard and securities repurchase rates unchanged at 2.50 percent, 4.50 percent and 3.30 percent\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  respectively.   Bank of Montreal    was down C$2.35 at C$57.75 after announcing on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Wednesday it had lost C$80 million after taxes in junk bond trading since\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  August.   ($1=$1.53 Canadian)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Jason Hopps, Toronto Reuters Newsroom, 416 941 8100, fax 416 869 3436, email: toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9h01bkr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
