\par\par \b \uc2  Toronto stocks shed 2.7 pct on Greenspan, Chretien. \b0\par\par\uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1998\par 17:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab     CHANGE\tab \tab \tab   CHANGE\par TSE300     5748.44 -157.61 HI 5748.44\tab    LO 5748.44\par DJIA\tab 7900.03 -189.75 GOLD (LONDON)   US$287.65 -0.60\par FTSE\tab 5105.20 -186.50 GOLD (COMEX)   US$293.60 +3.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NIKKEI    13859.14 -338.56 LME CASH NICKEL US$3980    -38 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANDLR\tab 1.5241\tab  LME CASH ALUM   US$1321.5  -4.0\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CAN 30-YR   137.16   +0.59 BRENT CRUDE    US$13.50  +0.24\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------MARKET COMMENT-------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Toronto stocks fell 2.67 percent on Thursday after Federal\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan killed hopes for a near-term\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par coordinated rate cut by industrialised nations. * The Canadian\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollar continued its fall after Prime Minister Jean Chretien on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wednesday called for a cut in U.S. and Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par rates for the sake of the global economy. * European stocks\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par were battered by losses in Asian equities, both tracing their\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par roots back to Greenspan's testimony. * Volume was heavy at 7.5\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par million worth C$138 million.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Toronto stocks shed 2.7 percent within minutes of the opening bell on Thursday after Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan torpedoed market hopes on Wednesday for a imminent rate cut by industrialised nations and Prime Minister Jean Chretien said he wished for a rate cut. "Chretien has a knack for saying the wrong thing and the guys in the U.S. love to blow the (Canadian) dollar up when he talks," said Joe Canavan, president of Synergy Mutual Funds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's dollar dropped sharply against the U.S. dollar after Chretien's comments on Wednesday afternoon and continued to fall today prompting the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy Canadian dollars as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traded between C$1.5210 ($0.6574) and C$1.5240 ($0.6561).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Toronto Stock Exchange's benchmark 300 Composite Index dropped 157.61 points or 2.67 percent to 5748.44. Early volume was heavy at 7.5 million shares worth C$138 million. Declines more than tripled gains 346 to 99 with 125 issues unchanged.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In New York, the Dow Jones Industrial Average fell 189.75 points or 2.35 percent to 7900.03 also on Greenspan's dashing of rate-cut hopes.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I don't think we'll get a rate cut at (next week's Fed) meeting. It'll probably happen late this year or early in 1999," Canavan said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par London, Paris, Frankfurt and Milan equities markets were dragged down after Tokyo dropped two percent to a 12-year low and Hong Kong posted steep losses after Greenspan's remarks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Germany's Bundesbank said it will keep its discount, Lombard and securities repurchase rates unchanged at 2.50 percent, 4.50 percent and 3.30 percent respectively. European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Wim Duisenberg said in a speech in Dresden that deflation was incompatible with price stability and the ECB's interest rate policy would be "no more restrictive than this target requires."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par All of the TSE 300 subindexes traded lower except for gold and precious metals. The heavyweight financial services subindex led the way, down 4.24 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bank of Montreal  shed C$3 or 4.94 percent to C$57.75 after announcing it had lost C$80 million after taxes in junk bond trading since August.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Northern Telecom Ltd.  was the third most active stock, falling C$3.80 to C$65 on general weakness in the telecommunications and high technology sector following Greenspan's testimony and sentiments in some quarters that Tuesday's announcement that Nortel would cut 4 percent of its global staff was proof it had overextended itself in recent acquisitions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ($1=$1.52 Canadian)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Gilbert Le Gras, Toronto Reuters Newsroom, 416 941 8100, fax 416 869 3436, email: toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9h01aqe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
