\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  dlr opens weaker,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1998\par 15:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar opened weaker at C$1.5159 (US$0.6596) in steady trading on Thursday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to suffer fallout from comments by U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan and Canada's Prime Minister Jean Chretien, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Greenspan failed to signal a desire for higher U.S. interest rates during testimony to U.S. lawmakers on Wednesday, while Chretien called for lower Canadian interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "We may be seeing some of the continuing impact of Greenspan's speech suggesting that a cut in interest rates was imminent. The optimism of a Fed cut may have been helping the Canadian dollar," said Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist with the Bank of Montreal. \par \par \uc2  "We're also seeing some fallout from Chretien's comments which have weighed on the Canada (dollar) a bit," Ferley added. \par \par \uc2  Markets had speculated lower U.S. interest rates would be required to counter fears that global financial turmoil would dramatically slow U.S. economic growth. \par \par \uc2  The prospect of a U.S. rate cut, which makes some foreign higher-yielding investments in countries like Canada more attractive, had underpinned confidence in the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  The release of Canadian trade data also failed to underpin the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Statistics Canada reported Canada's merchandise trade surplus was C$1.55 billion in July in line with a consensus forecast of C$1.5 billion. \par \par \uc2  The government agency also sharply revised down the June surplus to C$0.9 billion from C$1.54 billion to take into account a large shipment of precious metals and alloys that should not have been included among Canadian exports. \par \par \uc2  The falling Canadian dollar prompted the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, buying Canadian dollars as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded between C$1.5210 (US$0.6574) and C$1.5240 (US$0.6561). \par \par \uc2  "They've been in all the way up," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a Canadian bank. \par \par \uc2  In cross trading, the Canadian dollar fell to 1.1013 German marks from Wednesday's close of 1.1168 German marks and dipped to 86.76 yen from 88.53 yen at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Simao, Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8104) or email: toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony to the House Banking Committee later today for further signs the Fed will soon move to cut interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Federal Reserve could cut rates at its Federal Open Market Committee meeting on September 29. \par \par \uc2  Lower U.S. interest rates would make some U.S. investments less attractive to short-term investors, who then might seek out higher-yielding investments in countries like Canada. \par \par \uc2  "I think everybody is on hold waiting for Greenspan to see what he has to say and how much of an emphasis he'll put on a rate cut and whether it is immediate or a couple of months down the road," said Mario Angastiniotis, economist with Standard & Poor's MMS International. \par \par \uc2  Statistics Canada will release key Canadian trade data on Thursday. A Reuters survey of economists predicted Canada's merchandise trade surplus would dip to C$1.5 billion in July from C$1.54 billion in the previous month. \par \par \uc2  In cross trading, the Canadian dollar rose to to 1.1264 German marks from Tuesday's close of 1.1180 German marks and climbed to 89.95 yen from 88.09 yen at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Simao, Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8104) or email: toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9h01a5n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
