\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -2: Repeated  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 317 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 1998\par 15:07\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Thursday morning, selling U.S.\par dollars at C$1.5210 in an effort to smooth out trading\par action, said a trader in Toronto.\par    The U.S. dollar leveled off briefly after the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but edged slightly higher shortly afterwards\par and was trading at C$1.5216 at 9:01 a.m. EDT (1301 GMT).\par    The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was consistent with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par unofficial policy of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets when the\par U.S. dollar moves 70 ticks against the Canadian dollar either\par way on an intraday basis. The U.S. dollar closed at C$1.5140\par Wednesday.\par    In cases of sustained pressure against the Canadian\par dollar, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at every five-tick\par move of the U.S.dollar. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market sources reported\par follow-up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at C$1.5215 Thursday, and expected to\par see the usual pattern of recurring \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday if\par the pressure against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  persisted.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The action follows \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday afternoon, when\par the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold U.S. dollars around C$1.5090.\par    The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily in August, often\par departing from its policy of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 70-tick intraday\par moves, while the Canadian dollar was under severe pressure as\par a result of declining commodity markets and global financial\par turbulence.\par    Wednesday's action marked the first reported \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par in September. The Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bounced back vigorously\par this month, although some analysts believe it is vulnerable\par to global financial pressures and could once again head\par significantly lower.\par    After a string of winning sessions in notably volatile\par markets, the Canadian unit lost ground Wednesday after U.S.\par Federal Reserve Board Chairman Alan Greenspan told a\par Congressional committee that there were no plans for a\par coordinated rate cut among G7 nations.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  09-17-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0907EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4346 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du9h0276p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
