\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Afternoon: C$ Down As Greenspan Disappoints \b0\par\par\uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 1998\par 22:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Breaking a string of four consecutive winning sessions, the Canadian dollar finished down Wednesday on a late U.S. dollar surge after U.S. Fed chairman Alan Greenspan declined in his congressional address to offer specifics about whether U.S. interest rates will be heading lower. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.5130 at 4:30 p.m. EDT (2030 GMT), from C$1.5058 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT), and from C$1.5020 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets had firmed in recent sessions on expectations that Greenspan might hint that softer U.S. monetary policy might be in the offing, possibly as early as the Fed's next meeting on September 29. \par \par \uc2  But when his remarks to the U.S. House of Representatives Wednesday contained no reference to U.S. rates, many players in the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market were forced to unwind short U.S. dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people were short U.S. dollars and waiting for a big Canada rally to ensue (on the Greenspan testimony)," said Bill Mooney, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Societe Generale in Montreal. "But Greenspan said virtually nothing on interest rates, and that may have dampened the hopes of many who were looking for lower U.S. rates in a couple of weeks." \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar's decline began shortly after news of Greenspan's remarks hit the market, and by about 3:15 EDT (1915 GMT) had forced the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the C$1.5090 U.S. dollar level to contain market volatility. \par \par \uc2  The bank's action failed to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide, and traders said the bank was seen continuing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at each five-tick level as the U.S. unit broke C$1.5100 and continued climbing higher. \par \par \uc2  Mooney and others said the Canadian unit is likely to continue to drift lower, as there may still be scattered U.S. dollar short positions left to take back, and the fact that the Canadian dollar may be due for a period of correction after its own rebound following the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's August 27 interest rate hike. \par \par \uc2  "We came from C$1.5850 to C$1.4990 inside of two weeks," Mooney said. "Just on a very short-term technical basis, it would make sense for the U.S. dollar to rebound to about C$1.5250 before things peter out." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These are the exchange rates at 4:30 p.m. EDT (2030 GMT), 8:00 a.m.\par EDT (1200 GMT), and late Tuesday.\par    U.S./CDN    1.5130    1.5058    1.5020\par    CDN/DEM     1.1183    1.1272    1.1265\par    CDN/YEN     89.06     89.75     89.21\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 16-09-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2036GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du9g081lv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
