\par\par \b \uc2  Canada reserves plunge after big  \highlight22\uc2 currency operation \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrea Hopkins \par \uc2 732 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 1998\par 21:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (all figures in U.S. dollars) \par \par \uc2  OTTAWA, Sept 3 (Reuters) - Massive spending by Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the Canadian dollar drained nearly $5 billion from the country's foreign reserves in August, leaving it looking to borrow to replenish its stores. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Department said on Thursday a record $5.82 billion was spent last month on government \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , mostly market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  spending. The government offset a bit of the spending by selling some gold and increasing its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt, but reserve holdings still dropped $4.56 billion to $17.97 billion, their lowest level since last December. \par \par \uc2  The reserves, which provide a cushion in case of extreme exchange rate fluctuations and inspire confidence among international investors, had been climbing steadily at about $175 million a month since 1994. \par \par \uc2  Jeoff Hall, a market analyst at Thomson Global Markets in Boston, said he was alarmed by Canada's frantic dip into the fund. \par \par \uc2  "To me, what we had in August in terms of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  phenomenon was not even a rainy day....Yes those were dark clouds (but) I don't know that that was worth spending one-fifth of the total reserve portfolio in one month," he said. \par \par \uc2  Canada's dollar tracked commodity prices precipitously lower in August, sliding to a record low just above 63 U.S. cents by August 27. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fought a running battle throughout the decline, buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  13 days out of 20 in a largely futile attempt to prop it up. The bank finally resorted to raising interest rates to stem the decline. \par \par \uc2  "(The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) ended up being a rather defensive move on their part, trying to lean against the wind to no avail. At the rate they were going, they'd be out of reserves by the end of the year, and that's alarming," Hall said. \par \par \uc2  Jeffrey Cheah, a financial market analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS, said the the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's continuous defence of the dollar in August had prepared economists for a dip in the reserves, but the degree of spending surprised many. \par \par \uc2  "It certainly was a lot larger than what most people were expecting. It obviously reflects that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August was a lot more aggressive than what most people had expected," Cheah said. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Department did not disclose exactly how much of August's $5.82 billion in government spending went to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but one official said he expected most commentators who follow the reserve holdings would peg \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "in the $5 billion range". \par \par \uc2  The previous record for spending on government \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  was set in September 1992, during the European Rate Mechanism crisis, when Canada spent $2.9 billion and reserves dropped $4.3 billion. \par \par \uc2  In its release, Finance pledged to rebuild its stockpile of reserves and position itself to weather future market turmoil. \par \par \uc2  As a safeguard against a future run in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Canada said on Wednesday it had extended its $6 billion standby line of credit with international banks. \par \par \uc2  Cheah said Canada should have no trouble borrowing in international markets to build the reserve account back up to about $25 billion, or just over 4 percent of GDP. \par \par \uc2  "That kind of paper (Canadian debt issues) should probably be very well received, just because a lot of people might want to diversify their portfolio and a Canadian Euro market borrowing would be considered a good credit risk," Cheah said. \par \par \uc2  But Hall said returning reserves to July's enviable $22.52 billion level will take months, if not years. And he frowned on the two most obvious options for refilling the reserves - selling gold or borrowing internationally. \par \par \uc2  "I'm not sure that this would be an appropriate long-term strategy to start borrowing against the future here.... And the practice of using gold sales to replenish the coffers (is) a caution flag for me," Hall said, comparing it with a debtor selling land - it brings a quick profit but a hard asset is gone for good once the sale is made. \par \par \uc2  Canada sold 296,000 ounces of gold in July and August, the first sales from its gold holdings since October 1996, boosting reserves by $99.4 million. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.54 Canadian) \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Ottawa Newsroom 613-235-6745, fax 613-235-5890)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021217du9300qcq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
