\par\par \b \uc2  Toronto stocks caught up in world wide slump. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Scott Anderson \par \uc2 849 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 1998\par 00:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 31 (Reuters) - The bear continued to devour investors on Monday as uncertainties in international markets and a slumping Canadian dollar fed the beast's appetite for destruction. \par \par \uc2  North American markets proved to be the main course on the bear's plate. \par \par \uc2  In Toronto, the key 300 Composite Index dived 235.67 points or 4.1 percent to 5530.64. Volume was a light 98.2 million shares worth C$1.79 billion. Declining stocks smothered advancers 855 to 261 with another 254 issues unchanged on the sidelines. \par \par \uc2  With today's gut wrenching loss, the Toronto market has plunged 2291.61 points from its April high. \par \par \uc2  And in New York, the bellwether Dow Jones Index Average suffered its second-biggest point loss dropping 512.61 points or 6.37 percent to 7539.07. \par \par \uc2  The technology-heavy Nasdaq also demonstrated the compounding problems as it posted its biggest point loss in history and fourth largest percentage decline, falling 140.53 points or 8.57 percent to 1499.155. \par \par \uc2  As has been the case often in this topsy-turvy market, the Toronto market began the day on a positive note climbing 49.14 points to 5815.45 where it hovered for much of the day before taking a shocking and destructive turn late in the day, when North American markets began their steady descent at breakneck speed. \par \par \uc2  In the last hour of trading alone, the TSE 300 lost more than two percent of its value. \par \par \uc2  "I haven't seen any news to explain that. It's just a mystery as to why this thing has fallen out of bed so fast on so little news. It just doesn't happen this way," said Conor Bill, director of retail trading at ScotiaMcLeod in Toronto \par \par \uc2  "This is bear sentiment turning. People just turned bearish. They looked at the weakness on the Dow and said 'There's no good news coming, take me out'." \par \par \uc2  Though there was nothing concrete on the day to force the international markets lower, the usual culprits - a slumping Canadian dollar, weakening demand for commodities, uncertainty in Asia and Russia, and concern over weaker than expected earnings from the United States - conspired to wreak havoc. \par \par \uc2  "This has been a pretty serious down day, it's being driven by its own momentum, there's no bid out there. A market will fall on its own weight. It takes serious buying to make the market go up," said Doug Davis, president of Davis-Rea Ltd. Investment Counsel. \par \par \uc2  Overall in Toronto, all of the TSE 300's 14 subindexes closed lower led by a 7.1 percent dip in the conglomerates index and a 5.2 percent drop in the communications and media index. The gold and precious minerals group and oils index also suffered dramatic losses. \par \par \uc2  The bluest of the blue chip stocks led the markets lower. Telecommunications giant BCE Inc. , which holds the most weight on the index at 6.9 percent, dropped C$1.80 to C$50.60 in heavy volume of 3.6 million shares. Northern Telecom Ltd. , the third-highest weighted stock on the index at 4 percent dipped C$3.35 to C$75.25 and Bank of Nova Scotia  fell C$0.10 to C$25.80. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar also closed weaker at C$1.5674 (US$0.6379) after the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the troubled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought Canadian dollars at levels from C$1.5675 (US$0.6379) to C$1.5730 (US$0.6357) as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened past key support levels. \par \par \uc2  Following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Canadian dollar stabilised and was eventually sidelined by falling equity markets. \par \par \uc2  As is the trend, with the devastation in the equity markets, Canadian bonds rallied for a second day, as investors fled falling shares for the safety of bonds. \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond gained C$1.25 in value to trade at C$131.75 and yield 5.732 percent. \par \par \uc2  The difference in yields between the Canadian and U.S. 30-year bonds was 46 basis points, unchanged from Friday but down from 52 basis points on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even though we're trading at weaker levels, has seen some buying interest over the past one or two sessions," Rob Palombi, a senior fixed-income analyst with MMS International told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "(The gain in bonds ) was just safe-haven interest on the back of the slide in stocks." \par \par \uc2  Analysts now fear the worst for the mutual fund industry where the majority of Canadians have their money invested. With massive losses people are now reconsidering their portfolios, seeking ways to stop the losses. \par \par \uc2  "These days are very disturbing because this is going to cause a lot of contraction in the mutual fund sector. This is where people get worried and redeem their funds. This will start some redemption activity." \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.57 Canadian) \par \par \uc2  ((Scott Anderson, Reuters Toronto Newsroom, 416 941-8106, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8v01uiq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
