\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -2: Follows Usual Policy \b0\par\par\uc2 309 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 1998\par 17:34\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Monday morning, selling U.S.\par dollars at C$1.5675 in an effort to smooth out trading\par action, said a trader in Toronto.\par    Other sources reported the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par at C$1.5680.\par    The U.S. dollar leveled off after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par was hovering at C$1.5670 at 11:27 a.m. EDT (1527 GMT).\par    The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's unofficial policy is to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets after intraday moves in either direction of\par 70 ticks or more in order to contain volatility. The U.S.\par dollar closed at C$1.5605 on Friday, so Monday's action was\par consistent with the bank's usual policy.\par    In cases of sustained pressure against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the\par bank will continue to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  repeatedly at each five-tick\par level.\par    In recent weeks, the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has also experimented\par with various different approaches to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in an effort to\par defend the beleaguered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    The Canadian dollar rebounded on Friday after a dramatic\par drop on Thursday. It continued its upward push in overseas\par trading Monday morning, with the U.S. dollar dropping as low\par as C$1.5485 in Asia trading.\par    But U.S. dollar reversed its direction in volatile,\par illiquid trading later Monday morning, and climbed up over\par the bank's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trigger of C$1.5675.\par    The pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to last until\par the pressure on commodity prices created by the problems in\par Asia and other emerging markets finally relents.\par    Last Thursday, the Bank of Canada raised its\par trendsetting bank rate 100 basis points to 6.00% in an\par effort to stabilize the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but so far the move has\par had only limited success.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  08-31-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 1134EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4346 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du8v021hv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
