\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Raises Rates Sharply In Vain Effort To Defend Its  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1998\par 17:48\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO -(Dow Jones)- The widening global financial crisis hit North America Thursday, as Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised official interest rates sharply in a controversial attempt to support a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bedeviled by plunging commodity prices and global volatility. Canadian stocks plummeted. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada, the country's equivalent of the U.S. Federal Reserve, raised its benchmark bank rate by a full percentage point, to 6%, in an effort to alleviate the massive pressure stacked up against the Canadian dollar. Major Canadian banks followed by raising their prime lending rates. \par \par \uc2  However, the rate increase and subsequent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to stem the Canadian dollar's slide. In late trading, the U.S. dollar was trading at 1.5795 Canadian dollars, up from C$1.5655 late Wednesday in New York. On the Toronto Stock Exchange, the bellwether 300-stock index plunged 6% and the bond market also fell sharply. The yield spread between Canada's 30-year bond and its U.S. counterpart widened to three-quarters of a percentage point, from a half-point. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar had recorded new all-time highs against the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in nearly every session over the past week. The Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has suffered from fundamental weaknesses stemming from lower short-term interest rates compared to those in the U.S., low commodity prices and global uncertainties stemming from the renewed economic and financial turmoil in Asia and Russia. \par \par \uc2  These weaknesses were exacerbated in recent days by strong global flows of capital seeking the safety of U.S. assets in the wake of the Russian debt crisis and a consequent revival of intense speculative pressure. \par \par \uc2  Canadian Finance Minister Paul Martin said "Canada is being hit by a very nervous and uncertain international scene." He noted that primary commodity prices have been hurt by economic problems in Asia and Russia, and Canada's currrency has been affected. Some 35% of Canada's exports are commodity-based. \par \par \uc2  The finance minister claimed Canada is "strongly positioned to weather the storm," noting the country has eliminated its deficit and begun paying down debt. He said despite today's increase by the Bank of Canada, "interest rates remain low by the standards of the last 20 years." \par \par \uc2  Both the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of England \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Canadian dollar Thursday. However, some observers said the Bank of Canada's rate increase reduced the credibility and effectiveness of its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics. \par \par \uc2  "Whatever the bank's spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  today, trust me, they've spent far more in credibility," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. "All the rate hike did was give offshore accounts better levels to sell their Canadian assets. Accounts are making a lot of money today at the expense of inadequate policy decisions." \par \par \uc2  Many said the overwhelming pressure against the Canadian dollar won't ease appreciably as long as world-wide commodity prices continue to fall, as in the mind of the market, the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is closely linked to the value of commodities. There was speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may soon have to institute another, smaller hike in rates to try and stabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada had resisted raising rates despite the massive pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent sessions. In a statement regarding its action, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent slide reflected downward pressures on the prices of the commodity products Canada exports, and developing problems in Russia. \par \par \uc2  But the momentum in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline and upward pressure on long- and medium-term interest rates signaled a diminishing confidence in Canadian dollar investments, and that prompted the increase, the bank said. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think they expected it to be a resounding success as it was a very unpalatable option, but probably the only one open to them," said Mark Chandler, an economist with Goldman Sachs in Toronto. \par \par \uc2  Other analysts were more critical, saying the rate increase may end up inflicting more serious injuries to the Canadian economy. \par \par \uc2  "It's just a sad day for Canada and the 1.3 million jobless," said Sal Guatieri, an economist at the Bank of Montreal. "It's ineffective, it's not supporting our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and it's really undermining the outlook for our economy and investor confidence in our economy." \par \par \uc2  "The downside risks to our economy have hugely increased in light of today's rate hike by the Bank of Canada," Guatieri said, pointing to the fact that the Canadian yield curve inverted after the increase, meaning long-term securities had a higher yield than short-term ones. \par \par \uc2  Bill Mooney, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Societe Generale in Montreal, called the move an "unmitigated, unqualified disaster." \par \par \uc2  "They would have been better cutting instead of raising 100 points today. It's a total exercise in futility - people knew it was too little, too late, and they would have been better off not doing anything at all," Mooney said. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du8r017db\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
