\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian markets sink despite  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  rate hike. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Simao \par \uc2 735 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 1998\par 00:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 27 (Reuters) - Canada's financial markets suffered a bloodbath on Thursday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates in a futile bid to bolster confidence in the sickly Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Panicky investors dumped Canadian equities and fixed income instruments in step with the Bank of Canada's decision to hike its key rate by a full percentage point to 6.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian sell-off was intensified by a financial meltdown in Russia and growing international fears that an economic downturn in emerging markets could spread to more developed Western economies. \par \par \uc2  Russia suspended all trading of the free-falling rouble as its economy teetered on the brink of collapse and opposition politicians stepped up demands that Russian President Boris Yeltsin resign. \par \par \uc2  Moscow's deepening crisis prompted an international stampede to hold U.S. dollars at the expense of most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the commodity-dependent Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The fear that is there is creating a huge flight to quality from all markets to the United States and from equities to bonds. Canada, unfortunately, is being caught in that," said Michael Gregory, senior economist at New York-based brokerage firm Lehman Brothers Inc. \par \par \uc2  Canada's Finance Minister Paul Martin tried to reassure Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets, saying the country was "strongly positioned" to weather the economic storm. \par \par \uc2  "While these are turbulent times, we have no choice but to deal with them. We must not lose sight of the fact that Canada is strongly positioned to weather the storm," Martin told reporters at a hastily called news conference in Montreal. \par \par \uc2  "I have complete confidence in the governor and in the Bank of Canada and the action that they took today," Martin said, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must "act to maintain order and calm to the best extent possible in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets." \par \par \uc2  Canada's day of pain began in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market where the Canadian dollar fell to a new record low of 63.11 U.S. cents following the Bank of Canada's interest rate hike. \par \par \uc2  The troubled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , known widely as the "loonie" but recently dubbed the "northern peso", eventually stabilised at 63.21 U.S. cents on the back of heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy Canadian dollars. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's largest stock market soon followed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. The Toronto Stock Exchange's 300 stock index plummeted about six percent to 5799.46, wiping millions off stock prices across the board. \par \par \uc2  Analysts fretted that higher interest rates would hurt consumer confidence, draw investors out of high-yield blue chip stocks and topple Canada's slowing economy into a recession. \par \par \uc2  "You can't hold back a hurricane and the markets are essentially saying the same thing. The last thing this economy needs is dampening of consumer demand and consumer confidence," said Ruth Getter, chief economist with Toronto-Dominion Bank. \par \par \uc2  Canadian bonds also choked on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bitter medicine. \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond due June 1, 2027 fell C$0.76 to C$130.20 to yield 5.867 percent, even as the U.S. 30-year bond rose 24/32 to yield 5.37 percent. \par \par \uc2  The fiasco on Canadian markets was an astonishing embarrassment for the Bank of Canada which had vowed to hold interest rates steady until Canada's slowing economy broke free of the shackles imposed by chaos in emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last raised interest rates by 50 basis points in January in an equally unsuccessful attempt to bolster the weakening Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  In recent weeks, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had opted to support the local dollar with a short-term strategy of sporadic, heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued that strategy on Thursday, repeatedly buying the Canadian dollar as audacious traders drove the unit ever lower. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian unit has lost about 11 percent of its value against the U.S. dollar in the past six months. \par \par \uc2  "People sit up and take notice when a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  just falls out of bed like that," said Jeoff Hall, a Canadian market analyst at Boston-based Thomson Global Markets. \par \par \uc2  "Investor confidence has really been shaken by this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debacle that's going on right now in Canada. There's some fear that rates may go up again as soon as tomorrow." \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.58 Canadian). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8r01ofn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
