\par\par \b \uc2  Toronto stocks suffer worst drop ever. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sarah Edmonds \par \uc2 631 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1998\par 18:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 27 (Reuters) - Toronto stocks on Thursday suffered their worst drop ever after the Bank of Canada hiked interest rates by one full percentage point in an apparently futile attempt to defend the falling Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Gloom on global financial markets and ailing commodity prices added to Toronto's woes. \par \par \uc2  The TSE 300 Composite index went into a steep decline from the opening bell and had plummeted 502.70 points, or 8.14 percent, to 5669.60 by around 1140 EDT/1540 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Toronto Stock Exchange spokesman Steve Kee said the previous worst point drop was set on October 27, 1997, when the TSE 300 Composite Index closed down 434.25 points. \par \par \uc2  The largest decline in percentage terms was on the October 19, 1987 Black Monday crash, when the TSE 300 fell 11.13 percent. Key said no records are kept for intraday changes. \par \par \uc2  All 14 subindexes were at least four percent lower, with losses ranging from 4.41 percent for pipelines to 8.95 percent for financial services. \par \par \uc2  "We're a lot closer to crisis than I would have thought. I thought we'd have another year probably before we reached these proportions," said ScotiaMcLeod market strategist Peter Gibson. \par \par \uc2  Interest rate rises hurt stock prices because of the risk of damage to consumer confidence. Higher rates also draw investors out of high-yielding blue chip stocks into bonds. \par \par \uc2  "Rate hikes have a dramatic effect on the markets because people are willing to pay less for stocks when rates increase, for a number of reasons," said Todd Kappala, investment specialist at Priority Brokerage. \par \par \uc2  "The major impact is felt on bank stocks." \par \par \uc2  The substantial fall in the financial services index recently has created something of a vicious circle. Bank shares are the chief driver of the TSE and the banks themselves are dependent on strong stock markets for a good portion of their profits. \par \par \uc2  The financial services group was down 789.50 points, or 10.37 percent, at 6824.91. Kappala said he felt bank shares were getting to a level where they were "good value." \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate rise, while damaging to equities, had little impact on the dollar which resumed its fall after a brief hiatus, hitting a further historic low of C$1.5845 (US$0.6311). It was trading at C$1.5783 (US$0.6336) after sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  is leaning against a hurricane. They can't halt the slide in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all. The rate hike was just a pure technicality, there had been such a significant tightening in policy led by the market before the Bank of Canada hiked rates," said Harvinder Kalirai, a New York-based analyst at I.D.E.A. \par \par \uc2  Equity analysts said if the dollar does not respond well to this rate hike, forcing the Bank of Canada to move rates still higher, the peril for Canadian equities will increase dramatically. \par \par \uc2  "It's very dangerous because...if you push the consumer into recession in Canada, then it's going to be very painful because you really do run the risk of deflation," said Gibson. \par \par \uc2  Following the rate rise, several Canadian banks raised their prime lending rates to 7.5 percent from 6.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  Commodity prices were sharply weaker. \par \par \uc2  Gold slid sharply to US$278.50 at the London afternoon fix, matching its low of January 12 which was itself the lowest level since June 29, 1979. \par \par \uc2  "Everybody's operating in a vacuum. Suddenly everybody wants to get out the same door," said Irwin Michael, portfolio manager at ABC Funds. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.58 Canadian) \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto Newsroom, 416 941-8100, Fax 416 869-3436, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8r01nts\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
