\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar closes weaker after rate hike. \b0\par\par\uc2 392 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1998\par 23:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 27 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar closed at C$1.5816 (US$0.6322) in heavy trading on Thursday despite a move by the Bank of Canada to raise its key interest rate by a full percentage point to six percent, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The troubled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell to a record low of C$1.5845 (US$0.6311) before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy Canadian dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  unit's sell-off was intensified by a financial meltdown in Russia and growing international fears that an economic downturn in emerging markets would spread to the more developed Western economies. \par \par \uc2  Russia suspended all trading of the wobbly rouble as its economy teetered on the brink of collapse and opposition politicians stepped up demands that Russian President Boris Yeltsin resign. \par \par \uc2  Moscow's deepening crisis prompted an international stampede to hold U.S. dollars at the expense of most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the commodity-dependent Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "People sit up and take notice when a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  just falls out of bed like that," said Jeoff Hall, Canadian market analyst with Boston-based Thomson Global Markets. \par \par \uc2  "Investor confidence has really been shaken by this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debacle that's going right now in Canada. There's still some fear that rates may go up again as soon as tomorrow," he added. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada said it raised interest rates to shore up the weakening Canadian dollar and re-balance monetary conditions. \par \par \uc2  "Today's increase in interest rates is aimed at providing support for the Canadian dollar in order to bolster confidence, while preserving monetary conditions that will help to sustain the present non-inflationary expansion of the economy," the bank said in a statement accompanying the rate hike. \par \par \uc2  Economists were left scratching their heads over the rate hike. \par \par \uc2  "I'm really sceptical of whether this move in interest rates will provide long-term upward support for our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If our economy does tumble a year from now, I think our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be much weaker than it is now," said Sal Guatieri, senior economist with Bank of Montreal. \par \par \uc2  In cross trading, the Canadian dollar dipped to 1.1370 German marks and fell to 89.05 yen. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Simao, Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8104) or email: toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8r01ncq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
