\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Canada buys dollars after hike - traders. \b0\par\par\uc2 154 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1998\par 17:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 27 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy Canadian dollars for U.S. dollars from C$1.5630 on after a desperate 100 basis point rate hike by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "The bank came in at C$1.5630. They've been in all the way up," said one Toronto-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. "They have been in actively this morning." \par \par \uc2  The sickly Canadian dollar fell to another record low of C$1.5800 (US$0.6329) on Thursday despite a full percentage point hike in the bank rate by the Bank of Canada. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key bank rate to 6.00 percent, but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders decried the move as insufficient to stem the Canada sell-off. \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8100, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8r01n48\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
