\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervene \highlight\uc2  -2: Follows Rate Hike; C$ Steadies \b0\par\par\uc2 366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 1998\par 15:38\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market Thursday morning, selling U.S. dollars at C$1.5630 in an effort to contain market volatility in the wake of the Bank of Canada's 100-basis point raising of its benchmark bank rate earlier, traders in Toronto said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had only a momentary effect in halting the U.S. dollar's climb though, although traders said it had restored some calm to the market. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at C$1.5650 at 9:28 a.m. EDT (1328 GMT). \par \par \uc2  Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place only minutes after \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  relented in the face of overwhelming market pressure and finally led domestic interest rates higher to try and halt the Canadian dollar's recent freefall. \par \par \uc2  The 100-basis point raising of the bank rate initially sent the Canadian dollar surging, as the Canadian unit rapidly moved from around C$1.5760, its level immediately before the rate hike was announced just before 9:00 a.m. EDT (1300 GMT), to C$1.5590 minutes later. \par \par \uc2  However, traders said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  quickly regained a strong bid to begin moving back up amid extremely volatile trading, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar had recorded new all-time highs against the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in nearly every session over the past week, forcing the Bank of Canada's hand in leading rates higher Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has suffered from fundamental weaknesses stemming from lower short-term interest rates versus the U.S., low commodity prices and global uncertainties stemming from the renewed economic and financial turmoil in Asia and Russia. \par \par \uc2  These weaknesses were exacerbated in recent days by strong global flows of capital seeking the safety of U.S. assets in the wake of the Russian debt crisis, and a consequent revival of intense speculative pressure. \par \par \uc2  Many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers had begun to voice misgivings about the long-term effectiveness of the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics, saying they would likely be in vain unless backed by higher interest rates. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires  08-27-98 \par \par \uc2  0938EDT \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du8r01yyw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
