\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar drop raises specter of interest rate hike. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Simao \par \uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 22:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 26 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar snapped through a series of record lows like matchsticks on Wednesday, heightening pressure on the Bank of Canada to raise interest rates to restore confidence in the troubled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar, locally known as the "loonie", plummeted almost one full U.S. cent to finish the North American trading session at 63.87, its lowest closing level since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was introduced in 1858. \par \par \uc2  Derisively dubbed the "North American peso" by some observers, the Canadian dollar has lost about 10 percent of its value against the U.S. dollar in the past six months. \par \par \uc2  The sell-off was triggered by financial turmoil in Russia, which prompted a worldwide rush to hold U.S. dollars, the preeminent safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Economists said the fast unravelling of the Canadian dollar made it now more likely the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be forced to eat its words and raise interest rates to bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "This is very bad today. This is clearly a question of confidence which the bank said would be needed before they made the move toward a rate hike," said Mark Chandler, senior economist with Goldman Sachs in Toronto. \par \par \uc2  Bank of Canada Governor Gordon Thiessen had previously vowed to hold interest rates steady until the slowing Canadian economy broke free of the shackles imposed by turmoil in Asian markets and weak commodity prices. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last raised interest rates by 50 basis points in January in a largely unsuccessful attempt to bolster the weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Policy makers fear another rate hike could push the slowing Canadian economy into a recession. \par \par \uc2  Instead, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has opted to bolster the Canadian dollar with a short-term strategy of irregular but heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  It repeatedly bought the Canadian dollar on Wednesday as determined \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to record lows. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent an estimated $3.5 billion out of its $20 billion in foreign reserves defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August. \par \par \uc2  Canada's Prime Minister Jean Chretien said he supported Thiessen's strategy. \par \par \uc2  "When you look at the Canadian situation, we have all the fundamentals right," Chretien told reporters in Moncton, New Brunswick. "I have confidence in him (Thiessen) and confidence in my minister of finance." \par \par \uc2  Although analysts initially praised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a good counterweight to excessive speculation in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, it has failed to halt the general downward trend of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We're caught in a downdraft and this is beyond the control of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is just providing liquidity and wasting money," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a Canadian bank. \par \par \uc2  The increasing likelihood of an interest rate hike has battered Canadian bonds, which usually take their direction from U.S. Treasuries. Even as the U.S. market firmed on safe haven flows, Canadian bond prices fell on Wednesday across the entire yield curve. \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond lost C$2.09 in value to trade at C$129.83 and yield 5.844 percent. \par \par \uc2  The difference in yields between the Canadian and U.S. 30-year bonds shot up to 40 basis points as investors demanded a premium to account for an increasing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. \par \par \uc2  Even so, some economists are advising the Bank of Canada to stick to its guns and resist calls for a rate hike. \par \par \uc2  "I think it is a very costly move. The Canadian economy is weakening and the last thing we need now is a rate hike," said Ruth Getter, chief economist at Toronto-Dominion Bank. \par \par \uc2  She said the Canadian dollar could not escape the selling pressure afflicting commodity-based \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "At this point, you've got to batten down the hatches and wait for the hurricane to pass by," Getter argued. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.57 Canadian). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01kb8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
