\par\par \b \uc2  Toronto stocks seen tumbling amid Russia unease. \b0\par\par\uc2 383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 15:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab     CHANGE\tab \tab \tab   CHANGE\par TSE300(CLS) 6256.01   +7.16 HI 6329.40\tab    LO 6252.39\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  DJIA(CLS)   8602.65  +36.04 GOLD (LONDON)   US$282.50 -0.55 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FTSE\tab 5515.30 -139.10 GOLD (COMEX)   US$286.70 -0.20\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par NIKKEI    14866.03 -206.90 LME CASH NICKEL US$3995    -84\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANDLR\tab 1.5572\tab  LME CASH ALUM   US$1319   -11.0\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CAN 30-YR   130.84   -1.09 BRENT CRUDE    US$12.54  -0.14\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------MARKET COMMENT-------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Toronto stocks were expected to sink sharply on deepening\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par unease about Russia's future. * The Canadian dollar hit new\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par historic lows and global markets tumbled amid fears of a\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Russian economic collapse. * Dow seen down 100 points.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Aug 26 (Reuters) - Toronto stocks were expected to sink sharply at the open on Wednesday as global markets tumbled and the Canadian dollar sank to new lows amid fears of a Russian financial collapse.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's going to be another one of those duck days. The Canadian dollar is just awful," said one equity traders.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Everybody's pretty depressed so the buyers will just watch them go lower on the opening. They may come in later maybe but...it's freefall for the opening."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Tuesday, the Toronto Stock Exchange's 300 Composite Index added 7.16 points or 0.11 percent to 6256.01. Earlier in the session it had gained 77 points, or more than one percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Russian rouble slid dramatically overnight, losing 41 percent against the German mark, and the Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it would not continue wide-scale \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  because it was virtually the only seller of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for roubles.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The tumble in the rouble came on the heels of Tuesday's announcement of a Russian short-term debt swap that some analysts described as an effective default.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Safe-haven buying of the U.S. dollar sent the Canadian dollar spiralling down in early domestic action. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued to fall to record lows, triggering Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from C$1.5560-70 and later at C$1.5595. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was trading at C$1.5579 at 0913 EDT/1313 GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Sarah Edmonds, Reuters Toronto Bureau 416-941-8100 e-mail toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01jnh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
