\par\par \b \uc2  Canada policy makers scramble to halt dollar's slide. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Simao \par \uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 10:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 25 (Reuters) - Canada's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  scrambled on Tuesday to halt a painful six-month slide in the Canadian dollar as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders continued to press for an interest rate hike. \par \par \uc2  The troubled Canadian dollar, derisively dubbed the "North American peso" by some observers, has lost about nine percent of its value against the U.S. dollar in the past six months in step with weakening commodity prices and swirling financial turmoil in emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished the North American trading session at 64.50 US cents on Tuesday - its lowest close ever. \par \par \uc2  While edgy Canadians fret over the rising cost of imports and their declining standard of living, the architects of Canada's financial house have treated the falling Canadian dollar as one might a rabid cat. \par \par \uc2  Canada's policy makers, led by Prime Minister Jean Chretien, have refused to consider changes in fiscal or monetary policy to bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , nicknamed the "loonie." \par \par \uc2  Until yesterday, that is. \par \par \uc2  Chretien, one of the chief proponents of the do-nothing approach, suddenly reversed course on Monday with a pledge to support the Canadian dollar with a round of tax cuts in the federal budget next February. \par \par \uc2  It was the first time the Canadian government had linked tax cuts to the chronic weakness of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is trading at its lowest level since it was introduced in 1858. \par \par \uc2  But traders openly ridiculed Chretien's proposal as too distant to have any meaningful impact. \par \par \uc2  "The market thinks that Chretien has got his head in the sand. It's laughable. People are just raising their eyes in horror again," said Stephen Wateridge, managing director of corporate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with CIBC Wood Gundy. \par \par \uc2  "What are we going to do for the next six months? Go to C$1.6000 (62.50 US cents)?" Wateridge quipped. \par \par \uc2  That sentiment evoked a general feeling in the country that the Canadian government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have mismanaged the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. \par \par \uc2  Chretien at first flippantly dismissed the dollar's fall as unimportant and a windfall for Canadian exporters. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Gordon Thiessen has also been assailed for telegraphing months in advance his intentions to hold interest rates steady. \par \par \uc2  Chretien's vague promise of tax cuts yesterday followed weeks of heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent an estimated $3.5 billion out of its $20 billion in foreign reserves buying Canadian dollars in August. \par \par \uc2  Although analysts initially praised the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move as a good counterweight to a speculative attack on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to halt the Canadian dollar's general downward slide. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't change the momentum, it just slows the decline in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  a bit. It is also only effective when it is surprising and it is no longer a surprise," said Sherry Cooper, chief economist at brokerage Nesbitt Burns in Toronto. \par \par \uc2  Cooper agreed that an across-the-board personal tax cut was needed, but said such a move should be accompanied by "minor" short-term interest rate hikes. \par \par \uc2  "There has to be a fundamental shift to ease fiscal policy and tighten monetary policy," said Cooper, who has argued for an immediate 50-basis-point hike. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada last raised interest rates by 50 basis points in January to bolster the Canadian dollar, but the gains from that move were short-lived. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains reluctant to tinker its monetary policy again for fear of tumbling the slowing Canadian economy into a recession. \par \par \uc2  Many Canadian economists concur. \par \par \uc2  "Higher interest rates are not warranted because of the likely impact on growth at this point - it would probably slow it further," said Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist at Bank of Montreal. \par \par \uc2  "It's important to keep in mind that the depreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is coming about because of weakening in commodity prices. That's a shock we have no control over," Ferley said. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.55 Canadian). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01n02\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
