\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar slides,  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  steady on rates. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Simao \par \uc2 540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 19:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 26 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar plummeted to yet another record low on Wednesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ignored calls for an interest rate hike and continued its war of nerves with increasingly bold \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders. \par \par \uc2  The beleaguered Canadian dollar, derisively dubbed the "North American peso" by some observers, fell below 64 U.S. cents for the first time since it was introduced in 1858. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost about 10 percent of its value against the U.S. dollar in the past six months as commodity prices slumped and financial turmoil roiled international markets. \par \par \uc2  Russia was the catalyst for the latest sell-off. \par \par \uc2  The Russian rouble slid by more than a third against the German mark after a 5-percent fall against the U.S. dollar triggered circuit breakers on the Moscow Interbank \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  Exchange. \par \par \uc2  Russia devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 30 percent and suspended some foreign debt repayments 10 days ago in a desperate bid to inject some order into its crumbling economy. \par \par \uc2  Moscow's misery has prompted a worldwide rush by investors to park their cash in the U.S. dollar, the world's pre-eminent safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The Russian situation seems to be getting out of hand, so there is major nervousness and everybody is fleeing to the U.S. dollar," said Ruth Getter, chief economist with Toronto-Dominion Bank. \par \par \uc2  Getter said the Canadian dollar could not escape the herd mentality now prevalent in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. \par \par \uc2  "At this point, you've got to batten down the hatches and wait for the hurricane to pass by." \par \par \uc2  Canada's deepening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis has spurred its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to continue a month-long strategy of sporadic but heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to bolster the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada repeatedly bought the Canadian dollar on Wednesday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  progressively snapped through record lows. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has spent an estimated $3.5 billion out of its $20 billion in foreign reserves defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in August. \par \par \uc2  Although analysts initially praised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a good counterweight to excessive speculation in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, it has failed to halt the general downward trend in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We're caught in a downdraft and this is beyond the control of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is just providing liquidity and wasting money," said a foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a Canadian bank. \par \par \uc2  With \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fast losing appeal, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  faces a stark choice: allow the dollar to hit bottom or raise interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Canada last raised interest rates by 50 basis points in January in a largely unsuccessful attempt to bolster the weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears that another rate hike could tumble the slowing Canadian economy into a recession. \par \par \uc2  Some economists and business leaders have called on the federal government to immediately cut taxes to help spur the economy should the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  finally move to raise borrowing costs. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders, however, have vowed to continue selling the Canadian dollar until the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes its move on rates. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.56 Canadian) \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto Newsroom, 416 941-8100, Fax 416 869-3436, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01l6r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
