\par\par \b \uc2  Toronto stocks fall further on Russia, dollar. \b0\par\par\uc2 661 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 16:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab     CHANGE\tab \tab \tab   CHANGE\par TSE300     6185.54  -70.47 HI 6185.54\tab    LO 6193.60\par DJIA\tab 8510.74  -91.91 GOLD (LONDON)   US$282.50 -0.55\par FTSE\tab 5525.80 -128.60 GOLD (COMEX)   US$287.00 +0.10\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NIKKEI    14866.03 -206.90 LME CASH NICKEL US$3995    -84 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANDLR\tab 1.5579\tab  LME CASH ALUM   US$1319   -11.0\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CAN 30-YR   130.68   -1.25 BRENT CRUDE    US$12.55  -0.13\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par --------------------MARKET COMMENT-------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Toronto stocks dropped 1.13 percent early on Wednesday due to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par unease about Russia's future. * The Canadian dollar hit new\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par lows and global markets tumbled amid fears of a Russian\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par economic collapse. * Volume was 6.5 million shares worth\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par C$130.4 million.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Aug 26 (Reuters) - Toronto stocks fell 1.13 percent early on Wednesday as global markets tumbled and the Canadian dollar sank to new historic lows amid fears of a Russian financial collapse.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Russia and the Canadian dollar - people are just covering their asses here. It'll be overstated in the liquidity sectors, so we're looking at financial institutions and interest-sensitive stocks," said Craig Strachan, research manager at TD Evergreen. "The TSE 35 is likely to be sold."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The benchmark Toronto Stock Exchange 300 Composite Index fell 70.47 points or 1.13 percent to 6185.54.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volume was 6.5 million shares worth C$130.4 million. Declines outnumbered advances 316 to 119 with 164 issues unchanged.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Tuesday, the Toronto Stock Exchange's 300 Composite Index added 7.16 points or 0.11 percent to 6256.01. Earlier in the session it had gained 77 points, or more than one percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Russian rouble slid dramatically overnight, losing 41 percent against the German mark, and the Russian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it would not continue wide-scale \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  because it was virtually the only seller of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for roubles.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The tumble in the rouble came on the heels of Tuesday's announcement of a Russian short-term debt swap that some analysts described as an effective default.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par French President Jacques Chirac on Wednesday urged Russia to implement economic reforms, saying International Monetary Fund support would only work if these reforms were pushed through decisively.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Safe-haven buying of the U.S. dollar sent the Canadian dollar spiralling down in early domestic action. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  continued to fall to record lows, triggering Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at C$1.5560/70 and later at C$1.5595. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was trading at C$1.5590 at 1007 EDT/1407 GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Dow Jones Industrial Average shed 91.91 points or 1.06 percent to 8510.74.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Twelve of Toronto's 14 subgroups traded down with financial services, accounting for about a quarter of the index's weighting, down 2.74 percent. Gold stocks, however, were up 0.35 percent on slight gains in COMEX bullion values.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - HOT STOCKS -\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Potash Corp. of Saskatchewan Inc.  led declines down C$4.75 or 5.18 percent to C$87. Statistics Canada reported a five percent decline in net farm income in the first half of 1998, which may cut into fertiliser and input purchases.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Commercial real estate giant Cadillac Fairview Corp.  was the most active early on as 815,000 shares traded down C$3.65 to C$29.25 after Tuesday's announcement it will sell, on a bought deal basis, C$100 million in convertible unsecured subordinate debentures with a view to repaying its short term debt.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Newbridge Networks Corp.  was the second most active, up C$0.70 to C$37, after posting first quarter earnings on Tuesday of C$0.20 compared to C$0.36 and then a C$300 million deal early on Wednesday with SBC \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Inc. to provide switches.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ($1=$1.56 Canadian)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Gilbert Le Gras, Toronto Reuters Newsroom, 416 941 8100, fax 416 869 3436, email: toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8q01kqd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
