\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Afternoon: C$ Down Sharply In Heavy, Volatile Trade \b0\par\par\uc2 343 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 1998\par 22:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar extended its freefall Wednesday, plunging more than 200 ticks in heavy trading volumes as the waves of global turmoil continue to shred the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.5648 at 4:48 p.m. EDT (2048 GMT), from C$1.5549 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT), and C$1.5494 late Tuesday. It rose as high as C$1.5697 before the U.S. unit's momentum finally exhausted itself and the U.S. dollar began receding to slightly lower levels. \par \par \uc2  The bloodletting took place despite steady and sizeable \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout the session, traders said, with many market players now sensing that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become a doomed and futile tactic. \par \par \uc2  "The market is obviously disheartened," said Bill Mooney, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Societe Generale in Montreal. "People in this whole move downwards for the Canadian dollar never sensed that the bank has lost the war as much as they did today." \par \par \uc2  Mooney cited the extraordinary rapidity and scope of the Canadian unit's fall Wednesday, despite a rumored total of more than C$1 billion spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as a telling sign that the market no longer regards \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  actions as having the same weight as they carried even a week earlier. \par \par \uc2  Although the U.S. dollar has been on a strong uptrend against the Canadian unit owing to its safe haven status as global markets are shaken by the Russian debt crisis, many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers attributed the scale of Wednesday's fall to poor sentiment spread by falling commodity prices. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday's massive selloff of the the Canadian dollar also coincided with a fall of the bellwether CRB futures index to its lowest level in 12 years. \par \par \uc2  The index of 17 commodity futures prices fell to just above 198 points midday Wednesday, its lowest level since July 1986. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 26-08-98 \par \par \uc2  2055GMT \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8q07b7l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
