\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar hits another low, bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Simao \par \uc2 545 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 1998\par 18:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 24 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar fell to a record low of 64.51 U.S. cents on Monday as financial troubles in Russia and Venezuela added to market concerns of a rising tide of separatist sentiment in French-speaking Quebec. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar's decline prompted the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try and shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in money markets, buying Canadian dollars at several levels between 64.67 and 64.62 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily and regularly since August 7 in a bid to halt the decline in the Canadian dollar, which \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have dubbed "the northern peso" because of its chronic weakness. \par \par \uc2  Traders, however, questioned the effectiveness of continuing \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try and prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "If the bank is not going to hike rates then they should stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because they're just wasting taxpayers money. It's not doing anything," argued one Toronto-based money market trader. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost about 10 percent of its value against the U.S. dollar during the past six months. \par \par \uc2  Russia, once again, was the trigger for the latest Canada dollar sell-off. \par \par \uc2  Russian President Boris Yeltsin fired his entire cabinet on Sunday and replaced Prime Minister Sergei Kiriyenko with former Prime Minister Viktor Chernomyrdin in a desperate attempt to stabilise Russia's precarious financial state. \par \par \uc2  Yeltsin's move followed a recent 30-percent devaluation of the Russian ruble and a 90-day moratorium on some of Russia's foreign debt repayments. \par \par \uc2  Rumours of an impending devaluation of the Venezuelan peso also added to the deepening financial crisis in emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  Both Russia and Venezuela are exporters of key commodities, primarily oil and nickel, and their financial crises were seen as yet another reason to sell the commodity-based Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The Canadian dollar is falling alongside all the other commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Sal Guatieri, senior economist with Bank of Montreal. \par \par \uc2  "There is further nervousness about emerging markets given the situation in Russia and Venezuela." \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said rising political tensions between Canadian federalists and Quebec separatists were hovering over the troubled Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Canada's Supreme Court renewed secessionist talk in a landmark case last week when it ruled that Quebec did not have a legal right to secede unilaterally from the rest of Canada. The court, however, added that the issue was largely a political question and should be decided by a popular vote in Quebec and by negotiations among politicians. \par \par \uc2  Separatist Quebec Premier Lucien Bouchard quickly declared the court's decision a victory and embarked on what many observers view as a pre-election tour of the largely French-speaking province. \par \par \uc2  Markets fear that another separatist victory in Quebec would lead to a third independence referendum in the divided province. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar lost about four percent of its value in the month before the 1995 referendum, which Quebec separatists narrowly lost. \par \par \uc2  "Given that the Supreme Court has unwittingly given Bouchard a playing card, that's likely to be a source of trouble for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Mario Angastiniotis, an economist with Standard & Poor's MMS International. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8o01c7w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
