\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar ends at new low, bank steps in. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,078 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 1998\par 22:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.5350/1.5430\tab ASIAN CLOSE    1.5310\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  US$ SUPPORT 1.5320 RESISTANCE 1.5600  HIGH 1.5325 LOW 1.5273 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.5316 HIGH 1.5425\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.5314\par PREV CLOSE  1.5318 LOW  1.5319\tab HIGH 1.5320 LOW 1.5292\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FRIDAY, AUGUST 21\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: BEARISH, ACTIVITY: ACTIVE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada's dollar closed at at record low of C$1.5417\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (US$0.6486), around its level reached at midday after plunging\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 100 points (one Canadian cent) from opening. *\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  breached\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par previous record low of C$1.5355 (US$0.6513) set August 7 in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par mid-morning trade. *Bank of Canada stepped in to buy Canadian\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollars from around C$1.5360 (US$0.6510) up to C$1.5425\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (US$0.6483). *U.S. dollar firmer around 145 yen, 1.80 marks\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par despite Japan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fears. *Russia's financial crisis,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Latin American jitters providing support for U.S. dollar and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par North American bonds. *Moody's cut Russia's foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par debt country ceiling. *Prime Minister Kiriyenko told parliament\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Russia has entered serious financial crisis, while parliament\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par demands President Yeltsin resign.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Aug 21 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar closed the week much weaker, hitting new lows and ending at a record low close of C$1.5417 (US$0.6486), caused by technical selling.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  settled at the midday rate, which was 100 points (one Canadian cent) lower than the quiet opening.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Renewed speculative selling pounded the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  below the previous record low of C$1.5355 (US$0.6513) set August 7 in mid-morning trade, sparking more stop-loss selling.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tried to defend the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to buy the Canadian dollar for U.S. dollars from around C$1.5360 (US$0.6510), but failed to halt the slide.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was last seen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at C$1.5425 (US$0.6483), a new record trading low for the local dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The bank has been defending the "loonie" relatively strongly over the past two weeks since after shifting to more aggressive, preemptive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to counter speculative selling of Canadian dollars. But the weaker trend continued.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Full stop at the figure (C$1.5400) and took it up there. "(There were) no rumours, just stop-losses," one trader said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian dollar fell to 1.1654 marks after 1.1736 marks at the previous close here, but rose to 93.85 yen after 93.35 yen as the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lost ground against the U.S. dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the chart, there was little resistance to the U.S. dollar's upward march.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A seven-year channel shows little is in the way before the U.S. dollar climbs to C$1.5800 (US$0.6329), possibly in the next few weeks, said Mark Roberts, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  technical analyst at I.D.E.A in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Until we get up there, there's very little to hold the market," he said. "Obviously all the big figure numbers like C$1.5600 (US$0.6410) will offer some kind of resistance, but the next chart level is C$1.5800."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the downside, the U.S. dollar is well supported around the previous ceiling of C$1.5350 (US$0.6515), and C$1.5320 (US$0.6527).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders are watching the direction of the dollar/yen. Despite fears of Bank of Japan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the U.S. dollar rebounded to around 145 yen today after a brief fall to mid-141 yen on Thursday, and to 1.80 marks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Russia's financial crisis and general concern over Asia's economic slump provided underlying support for the U.S. dollar and North American bonds as safe-haven, but not the Canadian dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Moody's Investors Service cut Russia's country ceiling for foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt. Prime Minister Kiriyenko told parliament Russia had just entered "a serious financial crisis," while parliament demanded President Yeltsin resign over his handling of the crisis.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As the Canadian dollar remained weak, the monetary conditions index (MCI) fell to a new low. The Bank of Canada said today the MCI was at -6.88 for Wednesday. It was quoted further down at -7,048 earlier today.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par When the local dollar started the day at C$1.5316 (US$0.6528), it was stuck in a tight range and appeared to be a quiet day in the absence of major market-moving events and economic indicator releases.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In assessing the political risk of holding Canadian assets, the market is looking for more reaction from Quebec separatist leaders to Thursday's Supreme Court decision.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Quebec Premier Lucien Bouchard, speaking to reporters in Quebec City, said on Friday that the court ruling will not affect the provincial election schedule, but did not give any indication as to when he will call one.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders are seeking clues on when the next provincial election will be held and how it could raise the possibility of another popular referendum on secession.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Supreme Court ruling that Quebec has no right to unilaterally break away from the rest of the country had only a muted effect on the Canadian market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The release this morning of another bearish economic indicator for June had little impact on the Canadian dollar as market players had already seen a surprise plunge in June retail sales on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's manufacturers' shipments fell 2.9 percent in June month on month due to plant closures in the automotive sector related to a General Motors strike in the United States. New orders were down by 2.0 percent and inventory was up 1.3 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Reuters survey of analysts had predicted on average that shipments would fall by only 0.7 percent and new orders by 0.8 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Thursday, the Canadian dollar came under selling pressure in the morning on the June retail sales data, which show a fall of 1.7 percent against the consensus forecast of a 0.2-percent rise. This suggests consumers might be tightening their purse strings earlier than expected. Sales were still up by 1.6 percent for the April-June quarter from the previous quarter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's economic indicator calendar looks slim until August 31, when the second-quarter gross domestic product (GDP) current account data are due out.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8l01bhv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
