\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar opens firmer, slips on sales data. \b0\par\par\uc2 773 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1998\par 15:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.5220/1.5340\tab ASIAN CLOSE    1.5328\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US$ SUPPORT 1.5200 RESISTANCE 1.5355  HIGH 1.5353 LOW 1.5235\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.5281 HIGH 1.5353\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.5294\par PREV CLOSE  1.5334 LOW  1.5235\tab HIGH 1.5339 LOW 1.5242\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par THURSDAY, AUGUST 20\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: NEUTRAL, ACTIVITY: MODERATE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada's dollar opened slightly firmer at C$1.5281 (US$0.6544)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par on back of yen's rise vs U.S. dollar. *Initial gains to around\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par C$1.5250 (US$0.6557) were shed after bearish Canada retail\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sales data. *Canada June retail sales down 1.7 percent against\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par consensus forecast of 0.2-percent rise. *Q2 retail sales up 1.6\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par percent from previous quarter. *Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to support sagging local dollar\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par vs U.S. dollar, last seen at C$1.5341 (US$0.6518) Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Market awaiting Canada Supreme Court decision at 0945 EDT/1345\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GMT today on whether Quebec can legally break away from rest of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada, and Quebec separatist reaction. *U.S. dollar continued\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par to slide to mid-141 yen on growing Japan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fears.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Aug 20 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar opened firmer at C$1.5281 (US$0.6544) on Thursday, boosted by a gradual but continuous rise in the yen against the U.S. dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Trading was cautious and choppy ahead of the Supreme Court decision, due out at 0945 EDT/1345 GMT today, on the legality of any unilateral secession of the French-speaking province of Quebec from the rest of the country, and separatist reaction to the judges' opinions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In early trade the local dollar was also riding the wave of vague and unrealistic rumour that the Bank of Canada would raise its key lending rate to prop it up.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Then came Canada's bearish retail sales data and wiped out the early gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The June retail sales fell a surprising 1.7 percent against the consensus forecast of a 0.2-percent rise, suggesting that consumers might be tightening their purse strings earlier than expected. Sales were still up by 1.6 percent for the April-June quarter from the previous quarter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "That (the retail sales figure) really hurt. It was surprising. We were looking for a reasonably good number." said Steven Butler, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Scotia Capital Markets. "It takes the off-bid of a rate hike out of the equation."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Stephen Wateridge, managing director of corporate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at CIBC Wood Gundy Securities agreed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I thought the ex-auto component would be slightly better than the headline (number), but the ex-auto was actually down by two percent," he said. "A horrible number. Hardly a backdrop for raising rates."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For the average consumer in Canada recent rises in the mortgage rate, based on rising long-term interest rates in the bond market, means the cost of borrowing is already higher and resembles the effect of a tighter monetary policy by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he noted.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Douglas Porter, senior economist at Nesbitt Burns Inc said economists may have to revise down their estimates further for Canada's second-quarter gross domestic product (GDP) to below two percent at an annual rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The GDP and current account data will be released on August 31. Canada will also release June manufacturing shipments on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market is very nervous this morning, depending on what happens with (the court) ruling. I think anything over the C$1.53 handle, the market will be a little bit nervous that the Bank of Canada may step in," Scotia's Butler said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market daily since August 7 to stem any drastic slide in the local dollar's value against the U.S. dollar. On Tuesday, it was seen at C$1.5341 (US$0.6518).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders are looking towards a record low of C$1.5355 (US$0.6513) that was set on August 7.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian dollar edged up to 1.1741 marks from 1.1717 marks at the previous close here, and fell to 92.76 yen from 93.84 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian unit has been fluctuating in a range of 92-97 yen for the last two months. On a weekly chart, it has been on a general uptrend in the longer term since it bottomed out just above 60 yen in April 1995, when the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hit record highs against key \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8k01832\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
