\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar ends firmer, but pares earlier gains. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,057 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1998\par 22:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.5220/1.5340\tab ASIAN CLOSE    1.5328\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  US$ SUPPORT 1.5200 RESISTANCE 1.5355  HIGH 1.5353 LOW 1.5235 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.5281 HIGH 1.5322\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.5294\par PREV CLOSE  1.5334 LOW  1.5240\tab HIGH 1.5339 LOW 1.5242\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par THURSDAY, AUGUST 20\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: NEUTRAL, ACTIVITY: LIGHT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada's dollar ended at C$1.5318 (US$0.6528), firmer than\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par previous day's close but bias is towards record lows. *There\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par was no sign of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up local\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par dollar today. *Market unaffected by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Supreme Court\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par decision that Quebec has no right to unilaterally declare\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par independence, but that Ottawa would be obliged to negotiate\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par with Quebec if provincial referendum called for secession. *In\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par early morning trade, local dollar fell on bearish Canada retail\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sales data. *Canada June retail sales down 1.7 percent against\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par consensus forecast of 0.2-percent rise. *U.S. dollar rebounded\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par above 143 yen from mid-141 yen in line with rise in dollar/mark\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par triggered by renewed concern over Russia. *Russia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par \f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 \par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expects bank failures, which it says will not threaten\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par banking system as a whole.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Aug 20 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar ended at C$1.5318 (US$0.6528) on Thursday, firmer than the previous day's close but still flirting with the downside towards a record low.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders are looking at a record low of C$1.5355 (US$0.6513) that was set on August 7 as the next key target.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Key domestic and global events had little impact on price action after the local dollar had suffered initial selling on bearish economic data, which wiped out earlier gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There was no sign of Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  today.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "No sign of them. They're resting," one trader said. "Since the fiasco this morning, it's been fairly range-bound. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened off a little bit in the day and is expected to weaken off a little bit overnight."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market almost on a daily basis since August 7 to stem any drastic slide in the local dollar's value against the U.S. dollar. On Tuesday, it was seen at C$1.5341 (US$0.6518).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar was unaffected by the long-awaited decision by Canada's Supreme Court in the morning that the French-speaking province of Quebec has no right to unilaterally break away from the rest of the country.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In reaction, the Quebec government said an absolute majority of 50 percent plus one vote in the next referendum on independence would be enough to start the process of secession.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par President Clinton's announcement in the afternoon that Washington launched strikes against terrorist targets in Sudan and Afghanistan provided little direction to trading. Those countries are believed to be behind the August 7 bombings of U.S. embassies in Kenya and Tanzania.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In early trade the local dollar piggybacked on an overnight rise in the yen versus the U.S. dollar triggered by growing fears that Japan would stage \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop the yen's fall.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Then came \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's bearish retail sales data, which wiped out the initial gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The June retail sales fell a surprising 1.7 percent against the consensus forecast of a 0.2-percent rise, suggesting that consumers might be tightening their purse strings earlier than expected. Sales were still up by 1.6 percent for the April-June quarter from the previous quarter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian dollar edged up to 1.1749 marks from 1.1717 marks at the previous close here, and pared earlier losses and was quoted at 93.43 yen after 93.84 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The recent shift in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tactics of the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  towards more frequent, pre-emptive strikes at Canadian dollar selling is "a sign that they really don't want to start using the interest rate defence just yet," Michael Gregory, strategist at Lehman Brothers in New York told Reuters Television.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A higher interest rate, in theory, entices investors to Canadian assets with higher returns, but it is unclear how much a mere rate hike could reverse the loonie's downtrend.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Credit tightening would be also against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy when Canada's inflation rate is around one percent, at the bottom of the 1-3 percent target band, and the latest data indicate slower domestic economic growth as shown in the gross domestic product (GDP).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The GDP was flat in April, flat in May, the data we've had so far in June suggest that the economy actually shrank a bit, and July might not be much better. So you're looking at four months of virtually no growth, if not a slight contraction," Gregory said. "A couple of more months of that, and people will start using the odd word, recession. And I think that's something the Bank of Canada doesn't want to happen because that then spills over into confidence."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "That (the retail sales figure) really hurt. It was surprising. We were looking for a reasonably good number." said Steven Butler, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Scotia Capital Markets. "It takes the off-bid of a rate hike out of the equation."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Stephen Wateridge, managing director of corporate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at CIBC Wood Gundy Securities agreed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I thought the ex-auto component would be slightly better than the headline (number), but the ex-auto was actually down by two percent," he said. "A horrible number. Hardly a backdrop for raising rates."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For the average consumer in Canada recent rises in the mortgage rate, based on rising long-term interest rates in the bond market, means the cost of borrowing is already higher and resembles the effect of a tighter monetary policy by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he pointed out.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Douglas Porter, senior economist at Nesbitt Burns Inc said economists may have to revise down their estimates further for Canada's second-quarter GDP to below two percent at an annual rate.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The GDP and current account data will be released on August 31. Canada will also release June manufacturing shipments on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8k0165q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
