\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Afternoon: C$ Edges Up After Trading A Wide Range \b0\par\par\uc2 415 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 1998\par 22:32\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar is only slightly higher late Thursday from its late Wednesday levels versus the U.S. dollar, after an eventful day in which the Canadian unit broke with recent trends and traded a wide, nearly 100-point range without once drawing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.5315 at 4:30 p.m. EDT (2030 GMT), from C$1.5275 at 8:00 a.m. EDT (1200 GMT), and C$1.5328 late Wednesday. It traded through a wide C$1.5245-C$1.5351 range, but this volatility was largely confined to the early morning and overnight sessions. \par \par \uc2  In something of a paradox, dealers said flows were very light throughout the North American session, despite an onslaught of news events throughout the day that included a key Canada supreme court decision denying Quebec the legal right to seperate unilaterally from Canada, the Quebec government's reaction to it, Canadian June retail sales data, and the U.S. attack on alleged terrorist targets in Sudan and Afghanistan. \par \par \uc2  Perversely, the Canadian dollar failed to react much to any of the news, with the possible exception of the morning retail sales report, which showed sales down 1.7% in June from May, and which helped the U.S. dollar to rise off its overnight lows at C$1.5245. \par \par \uc2  "At first glance, we still appear to be status quo here," said Bill Mooney, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Societe Generale in Montreal. "But none of the news bodes terribly well for the Canadian dollar - the trend hasn't changed, and it's going to weaken. No two ways about it." \par \par \uc2  Mooney said the fact that the Canadian unit exited North American trading at the low end of its daily trading range will likely bode ill for its fortunes Friday. \par \par \uc2  He said it will likely continue weaker in overnight trading in international markets, with a fresh test of its all-time intraday low near the C$1.5355 U.S. dollar level likely in store for Friday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These are the exchange rates at 4:30 p.m. EDT (2030 GMT), 8:00 a.m.\par EDT (1200 GMT), and late Wednesday.\par    U.S./CDN    1.5315      1.5275   1.5328\par    CDN/DEM     1.1747      1.1755   1.1724\par    CDN/YEN     93.37       93.13    93.82\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 20-08-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 2032GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8k07403\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
