\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervenes \highlight\uc2 , sells U.S. dollars. \b0\par\par\uc2 191 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 1998\par 16:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 19 (Reuters) - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell U.S. dollars at from C$1.5321 (US$0.6527) to C$1.5328 (US$0.6524) on Wednesday morning, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been stepping into the market for the last week or so to slow the pace of the depreciation of the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar opened weaker this morning from Tuesday's close of C$1.5282 (US$0.6544). The market is testing its downside again as it moves towards the record low of C$1.5355 (US$0.6513) set on August 7. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar did not benefit from the yen's steadiness overnight. The U.S. dollar has been sliding this week against the yen as traders become more wary of holding long dollar positions at a time when Japan's top policymakers are suggesting that the Bank of Japan (BOJ) will not tolerate a further decline in the yen's value. \par \par \uc2  ((Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8j014uo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
