\par\par \b \uc2  Canada bonds open weaker on local dollar's fall. \b0\par\par\uc2 873 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 1998\par 17:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 19 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds opened weaker on Wednesday, under pressure from the weakness of the Canadian dollar and softer U.S. bonds, with the price of long bonds falling more than a full point in Canada. \par \par \uc2  What appears to be temporary easing of concern over Russia's financial system and Asia's economic slump have prompted selling of North American bonds and buying of equities in general this week. \par \par \uc2  U.S. treasuries, from which Canadian bonds take direction, have been softer this week as the U.S. dollar has continued to slide against the yen on growing fears of Bank of Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  "We are underperforming quite a bit in Canada. I think this has to do primarily with the continued weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Arve Bendiksrud, vice president at fixed-income research at TD Securities Inc. \par \par \uc2  "Even the U.S. market is weaker... We seem to have more confidence in the equity market for the last couple of days, whether fragile or not. Probably some of the asset allocation that had take place into bonds has been reversed, for the time being anyway." \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond (June 1, 2027) fell C$1.07 to C$130.80 to yield 5.788 percent. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. 30-year bond fell 11/32 to yield 5.59 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Canada-U.S. spread, an indicator for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk premium for holding Canadian securities, widened to 20 basis points from 17 points at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar opened weaker this morning from Tuesday's close of C$1.5282 (US$0.6544). The market is testing its downside again towards a record low of C$1.5355 (US$0.6513) that was set on August 7. \par \par \uc2  Buyers in the market also stayed cautious ahead of the Supreme Court decision on Thursday on the legality of any unilateral declaration of independence by the mostly French-speaking province of Quebec. \par \par \uc2  "It's unclear what the reaction from the Quebec government will be to the Supreme Court ruling. Until we know that I think the bond market will demonstrate preference for larger risk premiums towards a wider Canada-U.S. spread," said Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS. \par \par \uc2  The Supreme Court judges will issue their decision in a thick document at 0945 EDT/1345 GMT on Thursday in Ottawa. \par \par \uc2  Any rekindling of the war of words in Canadian politics between pro-unity federalists and Quebec separatists could also discourage overseas investors from holding Canadian assets. \par \par \uc2  This morning, the U.S. bond market came under downward pressure on strong economic data which suggest interest rates might higher down the road. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. July housing starts came in stronger than expected, rising 5.7 percent to an annual rate of 1.718 million units after rising a revised 5.7 percent in June. \par \par \uc2  In the money market, Canadian T-bills were little changed, escaping a sell-off despite the continued fall in the local dollar's value. \par \par \uc2  "We had a bill auction yesterday, which went more expensively than people had anticipated. So a lot of people who had tendered did not get filled and had to buy after the auction at more expensive levels," one trader said. \par \par \uc2  "The market has held relatively well even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has weakened off. We're in a very different mind-set than we were the the last time the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at this level," the trader said. \par \par \uc2  The market has stopped pricing in expectations of an increase in the official lending rate by the Bank of Canada. In the past many market players thought the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would resort to credit tightening to shore up the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Canada's three-month when issued T-bill yielded 4.97 percent after 4.93 percent at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  North American stock markets posted modest gains this morning, while the Canadian dollar remained depressed. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this morning, sellintg U.S. dollars from C$1.5321 (US$0.6527) to C$1.5328 (US$0.6524) to defend the battered local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar did not benefit from the yen's steadiness overnight. The U.S. dollar has been sliding this week against the yen as traders become more wary of holding long dollar positions at a time when Japan's top policymakers are suggesting that the Bank of Japan will not tolerate a further decline in the yen's value. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was quoted just above 144 yen this morning, down from around 147 yen a week ago. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara, dubbed Mr Yen for his influence on the market, on Wednesday continued to stage a verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , warning traders of risks of keep buying U.S. dollars. "Risks of the stronger yen for investors are becoming bigger. They should be careful about that. Chances for yen-supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are drawing near," he told reporters in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  ((Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8j01