\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar opens down before court ruling. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,035 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 1998\par 15:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.5270/1.5350\tab ASIAN CLOSE    1.5280\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  US$ SUPPORT 1.5220 RESISTANCE 1.5400  HIGH 1.5315 LOW 1.5277 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.5310 HIGH 1.5315\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.5308\par PREV CLOSE  1.5282 LOW  1.5277\tab HIGH 1.5314 LOW 1.5274\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par WEDNESDAY, AUGUST 19\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: WEAKER, ACTIVITY: MODERATE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada's dollar opened weaker at C$1.5310 (US$0.6532) ahead of\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Supreme Court decision Thursday on whether Quebec can legally\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par break away from rest of Canada. *U.S. dollar down vs yen on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par growing Bank of Japan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fears. *\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  July CPI\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par largely within expectations with overall CPI up 1.0 pct y/y\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par after 1.0 pct rise in June and core rate up 1.3 pct after 1.0\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par pct in June. *On Tuesday Bank of Canada stepped in at C$1.5303\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (US$0.6535) to check U.S. dollar's rise.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Aug 19 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar opened weaker at C$1.5282 (US$0.6544) on Wednesday ahead of the Supreme Court decision on Thursday on the legality of a unilateral breakaway by the province of Quebec.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The decision will be released in a thick document at 0945 EDT/1345 GMT in Ottawa on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar did not benefit from the yen's steady tone overnight. The U.S. dollar has been sliding this week against the yen as traders became more wary of holding long dollar positions when Japan's top policymakers were suggesting that the Bank of Japan (BOJ) would not tolerate a further decline in the yen's value.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar was quoted just above 144 yen this morning, down from around 147 yen a week ago.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I think the market focus is shifting back to the domestic situation. We could see a Quebec sovereignty risk increase after the Supreme Court ruling," said Rob Palombi, senior fixed-income analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Overnight you had a lot of talk about a possible BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prop up the Japanese yen... and that benefited other commodity-linked \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , such as the Australian and New Zealand dollars, but the Canadian dollar continued to weaken."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Eisuke Sakakibara, dubbed Mr Yen for his influence on the market, on Wednesday continued to stage verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , warning traders of risks of keep buying U.S. dollars. "Risks of the stronger yen for investors are becoming bigger. They should be careful about that. Chances for yen-supportive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are drawing near," he told reporters in Tokyo.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian dollar slipped back to 1.1771 marks from 1.1790 marks at the previous close here, and edged down to 94.29 yen from 94.75 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The local dollar weakened this week on safe-haven buying of the U.S. dollar due to renewed concern over Russia's financial system. Any rekindling of the war of words in Canadian politics between pro-unity federalists and Quebec separatists could also discourage overseas investors from holding Canadian assets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders are looking towards a record low of C$1.5355 (US$0.6513) set on August 7. If that level is breached, the next target will be C$1.5400 (US$0.6494).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "It's (today's weakness) disappointment over the bank's actions on Monday," a senior trader said. "Yesterday they deviated from their policy of metrical \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but the amount they spent was minimal and they had no impact at all."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The same players, the same bank accounts decided to sell the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  this morning at C$1.5315 (US$0.6530), so we're in a very, very precarious situation. Even with good news coming out, that's not helping us. The stock market did well yesterday, the yen strengthened to almost 144," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of Canada continued to step into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market this week to slow the pace of the depreciation of the local dollar, at C$1.5303 (US$0.6535) and C$1.5296 (US$0.6538) on Tuesday and at every five points from C$1.5240 (US$0.6561) to C$1.5255 (US$0.6555) on Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In recent trading since August 7, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been launching pre-emptive attacks on Canadian dollar selling, sometimes aggressively with large amounts of U.S. dollar selling orders, except on Monday, when it returned to its customary practice of waiting until the U.S. dollar moves 70-75 points from the previous close.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economic indicators released this morning had little direct impact on price action in the market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's wholesale trade fell 1.7 percent on a seasonally adjusted basis in June from May, marking the first time since the slowdown in 1995 that sales have fallen for two consecutive months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A Reuters survey of analysts had on average forecast a decline of 0.6 percent. The drop was predominantly due to shutdowns resulting from a General Motors strike in the United States.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's consumer price index (CPI), a barometer of inflation, rose 1.0 percent year on year in July, at the same rate as in June. The core CPI (excluding food and energy prices) rose 1.3 percent year on year after a 1.0 percent rise in June. Economists on average had forecast the overall CPI would be up 1.1 percent and the core rate up 1.2 percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Given the continuing slack in the economy, the greater risk is that inflation could move below the target band... as you eliminate the impact of the exchange rate," said Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist at Bank of Montreal. "We've had a fairly pronounced depreciation of the Canadian dollar, which is still putting upward pressure on the inflation measure. But that's only a transitory factor."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par For the bigger picture, Canada's second-quarter gross domestic product and current account data will be released on August 31. Canada will also release June retail sales on Thursday and June manufacturing shipments on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8j013js\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
