\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -2: Seen Checking Move to C$1.5350 \b0\par\par\uc2 393 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 1998\par 15:56\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Wednesday morning, selling U.S.\par dollars near C$1.5320 in an effort to smooth out trading\par action, said a trader in Toronto.\par    The U.S. dollar leveled off briefly after the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and was trading at C$1.5309 at 9:47 a.m. EDT\par (1347 GMT).\par    Wednesday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came as the U.S. dollar topped\par the C$1.5300 mark and started edging toward C$1.5350, which\par remains its all-time spot high against the Canadian dollar.\par    The bank's action was typical of its recent policy of\par random, asymmetrical \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as opposed to its previous\par unofficial policy of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets only on\par intraday moves, in either direction, of 70 ticks or more.\par Tuesday's official U.S. dollar close was at C$1.5282.\par    Traders said the bank sold only moderate amounts of U.S.\par dollars Wednesday morning, but the move was likely intended to\par signal the bank's intention of preventing the U.S.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  dollar\par from advancing past the all-time high Wednesday.\par    The bank's new strategy of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  early and\par unpredictably began on August 7, when it sold large amounts\par of U.S. dollars as the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was trading at the C$1.5350\par level.\par    While the bank has generally been successful in\par preventing a further decline in the Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par minimizing outright speculative pressure against it, market\par players in recent days have begun to have misgivings about\par the long-term effectiveness of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tactics.\par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has failed to reverse the declining trend\par for the Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and many feel the strategy is\par doomed unless backed by a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  move to lead interest\par rates higher.\par    The Canadian dollar has been under severe downward\par pressure in recent months, prompting the more aggressive,\par activist tactics seen recently.\par    Analysts say the Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has fundamental\par weaknesses stemming from low commodity prices and global\par uncertainties stemming from the renewed economic and\par financial turmoil in Asia and Russia.\par    The intense pressure on the Canadian dollar is expected\par to last until the pressures originating in Asia relent, or\par the Bank of Canada raises interest rates to defend the\par embattled \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires  08-19-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0956EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4346 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010916du8j020tz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
