\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  dollar ends weaker despite  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 1,088 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 22:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.5250/1.5320\tab ASIAN CLOSE    1.5260\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  US$ SUPPORT 1.5210 RESISTANCE 1.5360  HIGH 1.5295 LOW 1.5261 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.5290 HIGH 1.5303\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.5270\par PREV CLOSE  1.5263 LOW  1.5265\tab HIGH 1.5290 LOW 1.5258\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TUESDAY, AUGUST 18\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: WEAKER, ACTIVITY: LIGHT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada's dollar ended weak but stable at C$1.5282 (US$0.6544) after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par \f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 \par bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  capped the U.S. dollar's rise. *Trading was cautious as the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. dollar slipped vs yen on Bank of Japan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fears. *Market\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par awaiting more economic data this week, and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Supreme Court decision on\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Quebec issue on Thursday. *Bank of Canada stepped in at C$1.5303 (US$0.6535) in\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par morning trade. *Canada June trade surplus C$1.54 billion after downwardly\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par revised C$1.33 billion in May. *U.S. trade deficit narrowed by 8.9 percent to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US$14.15 billion from US$15.54 in May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Aug 18 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar ended little changed at C$1.5282 (US$0.6544) on Tuesday, stabilising after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the morning but still quoted weaker than Monday's close.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar was still well bid against the Canadian unit in the afternoon, but trading was cautious in the face of stop-loss orders and the yen's recovery, which, however temporary it might be, was checking one-sided buying of U.S. dollars.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The market wants to see some more data on Canada's economic health in June to be released this week, and is keeping an eye on the decision on Thursday by the country's supreme court on the legality of any unilateral declaration of independence by the mostly French-speaking province of Quebec.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market is bid, sitting here 60 points away from the all-time high (for the U.S. dollar). I don't think anybody really wants to try and take it higher until after 4:30, when the bank goes home," one trader said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Weakness returned to overhang the Canadian dollar this week as investors favoured the U.S. dollar as a safe-haven on renewed concern over Russia's financial system. Any rekindling of a war of words in Canada between federalists and Quebec separatists might also scare overseas investors away from holding Canadian assets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I can't see how either decision can be positive," the trader said. "It's not a big issue really, compared to three years ago. It (the Quebec issue) certainly has lost impact, but there's not so many reasons to buy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  right now."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite recent aggressive and pre-emptive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to stem the U.S. dollar's rise, the outlook for the Canadian dollar is bleak.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders are looking towards the record low of C$1.5355 (US$0.6513), which was set on August 7. The next target will be C$1.5400 (US$0.6494).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Tuesday, however, the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was marking time or even sliding. After rising to 146.60 yen overnight, it fell under 146 yen, then stayed below 145 yen in North American trade on growing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fears.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's top financial policymakers staged verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to check the U.S. dollar's rise towards 147 yen. Vice Finance Minister for International affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said the yen had hit bottom at 147 yen on the chart. Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa warned the yen's weakness could develop into a global issue.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian dollar rose to 1.1790 marks from 1.1742 marks at the previous close here, and fell to 94.75 yen from 95.46 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Economic indicators released this morning had little direct impact on the market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's June trade surplus widened to C$1.54 billion from a downwardly revised C$1.33 billion in May. A Reuters survey of economists had put Canada's merchandise trade surplus at C$1.65 billion in June after C$1.67 billion in May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I was a little bit surprised by the downward revision in May. It suggests that we could see a deterioration in the current account in the second quarter, and that's a bit disappointing," Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist at Bank of Montreal, told Reuters Television. "On net, exports have managed to hold not bad, so (there's) some good news on that front."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He said Canada's economic growth should stay steady around an annual rate of two percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Canadian dollar "won't receive any strong support from merchandise trade until August" because the negative impact of the General Motors strike will be fully reflected in the July data, Randall Powley, senior economist at Scotia Capital Markets, wrote in a commentary.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's second-quarter gross domestic product and current account data will be released on August 31. Canada will also release the July consumer price index on Wednesday, June retail sales on Thursday, and June manufacturing shipments on Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Over the near-term, a degree of political uncertainty and weak numbers are likely to maintain some pressure on the Canadian dollar," Powley said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. trade data, which were also released this morning, showed the deficit narrowed by 8.9 percent to US$14.15 billion in June from a record high of US$15.54 in May. The trade gap with Asia was growing as demand in the recession-hit region was down and Americans, blessed with steady growth in income, continued to import low-priced goods from Asia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Russia effectively devalued its rouble by as much as 30 percent and announced a 90-day moratorium on some foreign debt repayments on Monday in response to a crisis of confidence in the government's ability to defend the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Monday the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  conducted mild \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to buy up Canadian dollars at various levels between C$1.5240 (US$0.6561) and C$1.5255 (US$0.6555), but this came only after the U.S. dollar had moved 70 points from the previous day's close.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The latest strategy of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been to launch pre-emptive attacks on Canadian dollar selling, sometimes aggressively, with large amounts of U.S. dollar selling orders started on August 7.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8i0126y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
