\par\par \b \uc2  Defend Canada dollar with rate cut, economist says. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Simao \par \uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 23:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 18 (Reuters) - The Bank of Canada should cut interest rates, not raise them as some economists have suggested, to defend the falling Canadian dollar, a prominent Canadian policy institute says. \par \par \uc2  The C.D. Howe Institute's unorthodox strategy is the latest contribution to a medicine chest of prescriptions and remedies designed to treat the ailing Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost seven percent of its value against the U.S. dollar during the past five months as financial crisis in Asia and plummeting commodity prices continue to prompt a rush toward the safe haven of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Canada's dollar fell again on Tuesday to 65.40 U.S. cents as unexpectedly soft Canadian trade numbers and the impact of Russia's devaluation of the rouble triggered yet another move to hold U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  "What the plunging dollar really needs from the Bank of Canada is an interest rate cut," said William Robson, an economist with the institute. \par \par \uc2  "We would be well served in everything but the very shortest run if the Bank of Canada would readjust its focus firmly on the domestic economy and give us the kind of monetary expansion we need to grow the economy to its potential," Robson added. \par \par \uc2  That argument flies in the face of conventional wisdom which holds interest rate hikes as the first line of defence for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under attack. \par \par \uc2  Rate hikes usually make it more attractive for short-term investors to buy securities and fixed-income instruments denominated in a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and almost always leads to some immediate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar did, indeed, gain about three percent against the U.S. dollar in the three weeks after the last Bank of Canada interest rate hike in January. The benefits of that 50-basis-points rise, however, were short-lived as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  then weakened precipitously in early spring. \par \par \uc2  Robson blamed the January move as the key contributor to a subsequent slowing of the Canadian economy and warned that Canada was treading close to dangerous waters. \par \par \uc2  "A hike in rates large enough and sustained enough to make any difference to the dollar's performance over the next few weeks would probably also tip the Canadian economy into recession by year end," Robson argued. \par \par \uc2  Although some economists privately agree in principle with Robson's argument, most fear a rate cut would invite further selling of the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  Sherry Cooper, chief economist with Nesbitt Burns in Toronto, said speculators would "trash" the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered rates in an environment of pronounced \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness. \par \par \uc2  "It would be exactly the wrong signal and it just shows a total lack of understanding of how financial markets work. Every other country in the world is raising interest rates to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Cooper said. \par \par \uc2  Cooper, who favours a 50-basis-points hike in interest rates to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said tax cuts were the most appropriate tonic to spur economic growth at the moment. \par \par \uc2  Despite the calls to tinker with interest rates, \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has opted instead to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in money markets during the past two weeks in a bid to bolster the Canadian dollar. \par \par \uc2  It has spent an estimated $1.5 billion defending the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since August 7. Analysts praised the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as successful in stabilising the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though they said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not a long-term solution. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only works for very brief periods. You have to see a generalized shift in policy before it begins to have a sustained effect," Cooper said. \par \par \uc2  ($1=$1.53 Canadian). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8i011rr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
