\par\par \b \uc2  Canada bonds end weaker on  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  anxiety. \b0\par\par\uc2 695 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 22:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 18 (Reuters) - Canadian bonds ended weaker on Tuesday, extending mild losses throughout the day as investors became nervous about holding Canadian assets. \par \par \uc2  The outlook for the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is gloomy again as the financial turmoil in Russia and economic downturn in Asia are clouding global economic growth prospects. \par \par \uc2  "The Canadian dollar has really affected all the assets in Canada, stocks and bonds," said Sheldon Dong, manager of fixed-income research at Midland Walwyn Capital Inc. "For the foreign investor, there is not fun owning Canadian securities when you're losing money on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar ended little changed at C$1.5282 (US$0.6544) on Tuesday, stabilising after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the morning, but still quoted weaker than Monday's close. \par \par \uc2  "The pressure on the Canadian dollar is continuing on long bonds, and probably some further small deterioration relative to U.S. markets," Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist, at Bank of Montreal, told Reuters Television. "The key is the performance of the Canadian dollar." \par \par \uc2  Canada's benchmark 30-year bond (June 1, 2027) fell C$0.64 to C$131.78 to yield 5.732 percent. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. 30-year bond fell 6/32 to yield 5.56 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Canada-U.S. spread, an indicator for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk premium for holding Canadian securities, widened to 17 basis points from 15 points at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  Any rekindling of a war of words in Canada between federalists and Quebec separatists might also scare overseas investors away from holding Canadian assets. \par \par \uc2  Buyers were cautious ahead of the release of a ruling on Thursday by the country's supreme court on the legality of any unilateral declaration of independence by the mostly French-speaking province of Quebec. \par \par \uc2  The decision will be released in a thick document at 0945 EDT/1345 GMT in Ottawa on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Bonds are under minor pressure," said Katherine Beattie, senior technical analyst at Standard & Poor's MMS. "The U.S. CPI data came in as expected, but we've got a continued negative bias in the Canadian dollar, which is hurting our entire yield curve." \par \par \uc2  A rebound in North American stock prices could be slightly negative for bonds as investors who had shied away from plunging stocks last week might be putting some money back into stocks from bonds and cash. \par \par \uc2  Despite recent aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem the U.S. dollar's rise, the outlook for the Canadian dollar is bleak. \par \par \uc2  Beattie expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to break this week the record low of C$1.5355 (US$0.6513) that was set on August 7, and fall towards C$1.5400 (US$0.6494). \par \par \uc2  There was little market reaction to North American economic data released this morning. \par \par \uc2  Canada's June trade surplus widened to C$1.54 billion from a downwardly revised C$1.33 billion in May. A Reuters survey of economists had put Canada's merchandise trade surplus at C$1.65 billion in June after C$1.67 billion in May. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. trade deficit narrowed by 8.9 percent to US$14.15 billion from US$15.54 in May. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. consumer price index rose 0.2 percent, and the core CPI (excluding food and energy prices) also rose 0.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  Canada still has a high employment rate, above eight percent, and a low inflation rate, which is at the bottom of the Bank of Canada's target band of one to three percent. \par \par \uc2  Against this background Canada has no reason to tighten monetary policy at the moment. There may be a slight reason to ease the grip on money in circulation. \par \par \uc2  "But the given the pressure on the Canadian dollar, I think right now interest rates will hold steady," Ferley said. \par \par \uc2  In the money market, Canadian T-bills were weaker. \par \par \uc2  Canada's three-month when issued T-bill yielded 4.93 percent after 4.88 percent at the previous close here. \par \par \uc2  ((Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8i01155\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
