\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar opens a bit weaker in range trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 629 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 1998\par 15:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par FORECAST RANGE   1.5250/1.5340\tab ASIAN CLOSE    1.5260\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  US$ SUPPORT 1.5200 RESISTANCE 1.5360  HIGH 1.5295 LOW 1.5261 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CANADA OPEN 1.5290 HIGH 1.5292\tab EUROPE OPEN    1.5270\par PREV CLOSE  1.5263 LOW  1.5264\tab HIGH 1.5290 LOW 1.5258\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TUESDAY, AUGUST 18\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TREND: WEAKER, ACTIVITY: LIGHT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *Canada's dollar opened weaker at C$1.5290 (US$0.6540). *Focus\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par on Russia's financial markets. *Canada June trade surplus\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par C$1.54 billion after downwardly revised C$1.33 billion in May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par *U.S. trade deficit narrowed by 8.9 percent to US$14.15 billion\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par from US$15.54 in May. *Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sell U.S. dollars at various levels between C$1.5240\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (US$0.6561) and C$1.5255 (US$0.6555).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TORONTO, Aug 18 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar opened a bit weaker at C$1.5290 (US$0.6540) on Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The local dollar weakened again as investors bought the U.S. dollar as a safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on renewed concern over Russia's financial system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's advance was limited. After rising to 146.60 yen overnight, it stayed under 146 yen today on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fears.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's top financial policymakers have been staging verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to check the U.S. dollar's rise towards 147 yen. Vice Finance Minister for International affairs Eisuke Sakakibara said the yen had hit bottom at 147 yen on the chart. Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa warned that the yen's weakness could develop into a global issue.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In cross trading, the Canadian dollar rose to 1.1766 marks from 1.1742 marks at the previous close here and fell to 95.31 yen from 95.46 yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's June trade surplus widened to C$1.54 billion from a downwardly revised C$1.33 billion in May. A Reuters survey of economists had estimated Canada's merchandise trade surplus at C$1.65 billion in June after C$1.67 billion in May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "I was a little bit surprised by the downward revision in May. It suggests that we could see a deterioration in the current account in the second quarter, and that's a bit disappointing," Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist at Bank of Montreal, told Reuters Television. "On net, exports have managed to hold not bad, so some good news on that front."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par He said Canada's economic growth should stay steady around an annual rate of two percent.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. trade deficit narrowed by 8.9 percent to US$14.15 billion from US$15.54 in May.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Canada's second-quarter gross domestic product and current account data will be released on August 31. The July consumer price index is due out Wednesday, June retail sales Thursday, and June manufacturing shipments Friday this week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Russia effectively devalued its rouble by as much as 30 percent and announced a 90-day moratorium on some foreign debt repayments on Monday in response to a crisis of confidence in the government's ability to defend the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Monday the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Canada\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  conducted moderate \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, buying up Canadian dollars at various levels from C$1.5240 (US$0.6561) to C$1.5255 (US$0.6555), but this came only after the U.S. dollar had moved 70 points from the previous day's close.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The latest tactics of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had been to launch pre-emptive attacks on Canadian dollar selling, sometimes aggressively with large amounts of U.S. dollar selling orders, starting from August 7.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Masaru Sato, Reuters Toronto newsroom (416) 941-8107, toronto.newsroom@reuters.com))\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8i00zdz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
