\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar closes weaker in light trade. \b0\par\par\uc2 446 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1998\par 22:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 17 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar closed weaker at C$1.5260 (US$0.6550) in light trading on Monday as a crisis in Russian financial markets and concerns over impending Canadian trade data placed pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  An international crisis in Russia was the key trigger for the Canada unit's renewed weakness. \par \par \uc2  Russia effectively devalued its rouble by as much as 30 percent and announced a 90-day moratorium on some foreign debt repayments on Monday in response to a crisis of confidence in the government's ability to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Canadian analysts considered the devaluation particularly important because Russia, like Canada, has a large presence on world commodity markets. \par \par \uc2  The strength of the Canadian dollar is closely tied to commodity prices because the country's economy remains largely resource-based. \par \par \uc2  "The Russian devaluation renews concerns about emerging markets and commodity prices since Russia is a huge exporter of energy and metals and minerals. That's another negative for Canada," said Doug Porter, senior economist with Nesbitt Burns in Toronto. \par \par \uc2  The falling Canadian dollar pushed the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, buying Canadian dollars at various levels between C$1.5240 (US$0.6561) and C$1.5255 (US$0.6555). \par \par \uc2  "They've been buying all the way up," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The trader described the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as moderate. \par \par \uc2  But Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be less effective than the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive moves to bolster the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past 10 days. \par \par \uc2  Analysts had applauded the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for setting a temporary ceiling on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which few speculators were willing to challenge. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved from a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out gyrations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to aggressively defending the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It does look like \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was somewhat less effective than it was last week," Porter said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts were also keeping a close eye on key Canadian and U.S. data due to be released later this week. \par \par \uc2  Statistics Canada will report Canada's merchandise trade data on Tuesday. A Reuters survey of economists predicted Canada's merchandise trade surplus would narrow to C$1.65 billion in June from C$1.67 billion in May. \par \par \uc2  Canada will also report key inflation data on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  On the crosses, the Canadian dollar fell to 1.1742 marks from 1.1871 marks at Friday's close and dipped to 95.46 yen from 96.43 yen at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Simao, Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8104) or email: toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8h00yda\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
