\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar opens weaker in light trading. \b0\par\par\uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 1998\par 15:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Aug 17 (Reuters) - The Canadian dollar opened weaker at C$1.5217 (US$0.6571) in light trading on Monday after investors sold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the German mark overnight in overseas markets, traders said. \par \par \uc2  They said they expected the Canada unit to be sidelined in North American trading as markets focused on continuing crisis in Russian financial markets and the release of Canadian and U.S. inflation numbers later this week. \par \par \uc2  Russia effectively devalued its rouble by as much as 30 percent and announced a 90-day moratorium on some foreign debt repayments on Monday in response to a crisis of confidence in the government's ability to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The focus today is the Russian rouble," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with a Canadian brokerage. "There are also a lot of numbers coming out this week, so I think the focus will be on whether those numbers show further Canadian weakness." \par \par \uc2  Statistics Canada will report key merchandise trade data on Tuesday and inflation numbers on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's previous aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had set a temporary ceiling on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which few speculators were willing to challenge. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has moved from a policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out gyrations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to aggressively defending the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on August 7 has been effective in creating a more stable range for the Canadian dollar," said Rob Palombi, fixed-income analyst with MMS International. \par \par \uc2  "They've \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at many different levels and that basically says the bank can be in anywhere at anytime and it has derailed the more speculative activity that we were seeing prior to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Palombi said. \par \par \uc2  The strength of the Canadian dollar, however, remains tied closely to commodity prices because the country's economy remains largely resource-based. \par \par \uc2  In cross trading, the Canadian dollar fell to 1.1789 marks from 1.1871 marks at Friday's close and dipped to 96.04 yen from 96.43 yen at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  ((Paul Simao, Reuters Toronto Bureau (416) 941-8104) or email: toronto.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8h00xa4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
